/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/securityx.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("securityx", [["sx1",0,1,"A CISO wants a mandatory, leadership-approved document stating that all sensitive data must be encrypted, while the specific algorithms and key lengths are allowed to change over time without board approval. Where should the algorithm requirements live?",["In a technical standard that supports the policy","In the policy itself, signed by the board","In an optional guideline for engineers","In the incident response plan"],0,"Policies state high-level mandatory intent and change rarely; standards make them measurable (for example AES-256) and can be updated by the security team. Putting algorithms in the policy would force board approval for every crypto change.","Domain 1: Governance components",[null,"Embedding algorithm details in the policy means every crypto update needs board re-approval, which is exactly what the CISO wants to avoid.","Guidelines are recommendations, so making encryption algorithms optional would not enforce the mandatory requirement.","The incident response plan covers handling incidents, not the ongoing encryption requirements for sensitive data."],1],["sx2",0,1,"A security program must show which team is accountable for approving firewall rule changes, who performs them, and who must be told afterward. Which tool best documents this?",["A risk register","A business impact analysis","A RACI matrix","A data flow diagram"],2,"A RACI matrix maps each activity to who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed, removing ambiguity about approval and execution.","Domain 1: Governance components",["A risk register tracks risks, their scores, owners and treatments, not who performs and approves each operational task.","A BIA identifies critical processes and their recovery requirements; it does not assign task-level roles.",null,"A data flow diagram shows how data moves between components, which is useful for threat modeling but not role assignment."],2],["sx3",0,1,"A risk assessment finds that a customer portal has a residual risk score of 16 after controls, while the board-approved appetite allows residual scores up to 12. The business owner wants to launch anyway. What is the correct governance step?",["Launch, because the inherent risk has already been lowered","Adjust the scoring method so the residual score drops to 12 and fits within the approved appetite","Transfer the decision to the security analyst who ran the assessment","Escalate for formal risk acceptance by an executive with authority, or apply more treatment first"],3,"Residual risk above appetite must be treated further or explicitly accepted by someone with the authority to exceed appetite, and the decision must be documented. Reducing inherent risk alone does not make it acceptable.","Domain 1: Risk management",["Lowering inherent risk is expected, but the residual score still exceeds appetite, so launching without a decision bypasses governance.","Changing the score without changing the controls falsifies the assessment and leaves the actual exposure the same.","Analysts assess and advise; accepting risk above appetite requires an owner or executive with authority.",null],3],["sx4",0,1,"An online retailer calculates that a web server outage would cost $40,000 per event and expects it about twice a year. A redundant design costs $50,000 per year to operate. Based on ALE alone, what should management conclude?",["The control costs more than the annual expected loss of the outage, so management should reject it","ALE cannot be calculated without the asset's purchase price","The SLE is $20,000, so no control is needed","The control costs less than the $80,000 annual loss expectancy, so it is justified"],3,"SLE is $40,000 and ARO is 2, so ALE = $80,000. A $50,000 annual control that removes most of that loss is cost-justified.","Domain 1: Risk management",["$50,000 per year is less than the $80,000 ALE, so the control is cheaper than the expected loss, not more.","ALE is SLE multiplied by ARO; both are given here, so the purchase price is not needed.","The SLE is the $40,000 loss per event; $20,000 is not derived from the figures given.",null],3],["sx5",0,1,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover the costs of ransomware recovery and legal claims. Which risk treatment is this?",["Transference","Avoidance","Mitigation","Acceptance"],0,"Insurance shifts part of the financial impact to a third party, which is risk transference. It does not reduce the likelihood of the event.","Domain 1: Risk management",[null,"Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether, such as not storing the data; buying insurance keeps the activity.","Mitigation adds controls that reduce likelihood or impact of the event itself, like backups or EDR.","Acceptance means knowingly taking on the risk without further action; paying for insurance is an action that shifts the cost."],1],["sx6",0,1,"A SaaS provider will process your customer records. Before signing, you need independent evidence that its controls operated effectively over a period of time. What should you request?",["A SOC 2 Type I report","The vendor's marketing security whitepaper","A SOC 2 Type II report","A self-completed questionnaire only"],2,"A SOC 2 Type II report is an independent auditor's opinion on the design and operating effectiveness of controls over a period (often 6 to 12 months). Type I covers design at a single point in time.","Domain 1: Third-party risk",["A Type I report only assesses control design at a single point in time, not whether controls worked over a period.","A whitepaper is written by the vendor for sales purposes and is not independent evidence.",null,"A questionnaire answered by the vendor is useful for scoping but is not independently verified."],2],["sx7",0,1,"After a widely used open-source logging library is found to be vulnerable, a company spends days finding which of its products include it. Which practice would have answered the question in minutes?",["Running annual penetration tests","Signing all code with a code-signing certificate","Increasing the SIEM log retention period","Requiring and maintaining an SBOM for each product"],3,"A software bill of materials lists every component and version in a product, so a query can show which products include an affected library right away.","Domain 1: Supply chain risk",["Annual penetration tests find exploitable issues at a point in time but do not inventory every embedded component.","Code signing proves who published code and that it was not altered; it does not list the dependencies inside.","Longer log retention helps investigations but does not tell you which products contain a library.",null],3],["sx8",0,1,"A business impact analysis shows that the order system can lose at most 15 minutes of transactions and must be back online within 2 hours. Which pair of metrics has been defined?",["RTO of 15 minutes and RPO of 2 hours","RPO of 15 minutes and RTO of 2 hours","MTBF of 15 minutes and MTTR of 2 hours","WRT of 15 minutes and MTD of 2 hours"],1,"Recovery point objective is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time; recovery time objective is how quickly service must be restored.","Domain 1: Business continuity",["This swaps the definitions: data loss tolerance is the RPO and time to restore is the RTO.",null,"MTBF and MTTR are reliability measurements of components, not business-set recovery targets.","Work recovery time and maximum tolerable downtime are related BIA terms, but the data-loss limit is specifically the RPO."],2],["sx9",0,1,"A disaster recovery team wants to validate its plan by walking through a ransomware scenario around a conference table, without touching production systems. Which test type is this?",["Full interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Simulation with live failover"],1,"A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based walkthrough of roles and decisions. It is low risk and good for finding gaps in communication and procedures.","Domain 1: Business continuity",["A full interruption test actually shuts down primary operations and switches to recovery, which is the most disruptive test.",null,"A parallel test brings up the recovery site and runs it alongside production, which touches real systems.","Live failover exercises move real workloads, which goes beyond a discussion around a table."],1],["sx10",0,1,"A European company's customers ask it to delete all their personal data. Which regulation most directly gives them this right?",["GDPR","PCI DSS","SOX","HIPAA"],0,"The EU General Data Protection Regulation grants data subjects rights including access, rectification and erasure (the right to be forgotten), subject to exceptions.","Domain 1: Compliance",[null,"PCI DSS is an industry standard for protecting payment card data and does not grant individuals a right to erasure.","The Sarbanes-Oxley Act governs financial reporting controls of US public companies, not personal data deletion.","HIPAA governs protected health information in the US and gives patients access and amendment rights, not a general erasure right."],1],["sx11",0,1,"A multinational must keep citizens' records physically stored in their home country, and a cloud architect proposes a single global storage bucket in another region for cost savings. Which concern should block the design?",["Data sovereignty and localization requirements","Right to audit clauses","Separation of duties","Non-repudiation"],0,"Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country where it is stored, and some laws require it to stay in-country. A single foreign region would violate that.","Domain 1: Compliance",[null,"Right to audit clauses let a customer inspect a vendor's controls; they do not dictate where data is stored.","Separation of duties splits critical tasks between people and does not relate to storage location.","Non-repudiation proves who performed an action, typically with digital signatures, and is unrelated to data location."],3],["sx12",0,1,"A company wants a certifiable information security management system that an accredited auditor can certify. Which standard should it adopt?",["NIST CSF","ISO/IEC 27001","CIS Controls","ISO/IEC 27002"],1,"ISO/IEC 27001 defines the requirements for an ISMS, and organizations can be certified against it by accredited bodies. ISO/IEC 27002 gives control guidance but is not a certification standard.","Domain 1: Frameworks",["The NIST Cybersecurity Framework is a voluntary outcome framework; organizations do not receive an accredited certification against it.",null,"CIS Controls are a prioritized set of safeguards; they are not an ISMS standard with accredited certification.","ISO/IEC 27002 is guidance on implementing controls and cannot itself be certified against."],1],["sx13",0,1,"A US federal contractor must select and document security controls for a system from a large catalog organized by families such as AC, AU and IR. Which publication provides this catalog?",["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61","ISO/IEC 27701","CSA STAR"],0,"NIST SP 800-53 is the control catalog with families such as Access Control (AC), Audit and Accountability (AU) and Incident Response (IR).","Domain 1: Frameworks",[null,"NIST SP 800-61 is the computer security incident handling guide, not a control catalog.","ISO/IEC 27701 extends ISO 27001 for privacy information management; it does not use AC/AU/IR families.","CSA STAR is a cloud provider assurance registry based on the Cloud Controls Matrix, not the federal control catalog."],1],["sx14",0,1,"Legal counsel informs IT that litigation is expected over a former employee's actions. Which action should IT take first regarding that employee's mailbox and files?",["Delete them under the normal retention schedule","Export them to a PST file and email copies to the whole legal team for review","Place them under a legal hold so retention policies cannot delete them","Reset the mailbox password and give it to HR"],2,"When litigation is reasonably anticipated, relevant data must be preserved. A legal hold suspends normal deletion so evidence is not destroyed (spoliation).","Domain 1: Legal and privacy",["Deleting data once litigation is anticipated can be treated as spoliation of evidence, with serious legal consequences.","Emailing copies spreads sensitive data, breaks chain of custody and does not stop the originals from being deleted.",null,"Sharing a reset password with HR undermines accountability and does not preserve the data from retention deletion."],2],["sx15",0,1,"During design review of a new payment API, the team goes through each element of the data flow diagram asking about spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege. Which method are they using?",["STRIDE","PASTA","DREAD","MITRE ATT&CK"],0,"STRIDE is a threat categorization mnemonic applied to each element of a data flow diagram at design time.","Domain 1: Threat modeling",[null,"PASTA is a seven-stage, risk-centric process that aligns threats to business impact rather than a per-element category checklist.","DREAD is a scoring model for rating threats (damage, reproducibility and so on), not a set of threat categories.","MITRE ATT&CK catalogs observed adversary tactics and techniques; it is not the six-category design checklist described."],2],["sx16",0,1,"A threat modeling team wants to reason about how real adversary groups have achieved persistence and lateral movement in similar companies, based on observed behavior. Which resource fits best?",["STRIDE","MITRE ATT&CK","CVSS","OWASP ASVS"],1,"ATT&CK is a knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques observed in real intrusions, including groups known to use them.","Domain 1: Threat modeling",["STRIDE categorizes design threats but does not describe how real adversary groups actually behaved.",null,"CVSS scores the severity of individual vulnerabilities, not adversary behavior across an intrusion.","OWASP ASVS is a list of application security verification requirements, not a catalog of observed attacker techniques."],2],["sx17",0,1,"Employees have started pasting customer contracts into a public generative AI chatbot to summarize them. What should the security team put in place first?",["A block on all internet access for the legal department and every other team handling contracts","An annual penetration test of the chatbot vendor","An AI acceptable use policy naming approved tools and allowed data, enforced with DLP","Longer passwords for employee accounts"],2,"Governance comes first: define which AI tools are approved and what data may be used, then enforce it with technical controls such as DLP or CASB policies. Blocking all internet access is disproportionate.","Domain 1: AI adoption",["Cutting off all internet access disrupts the business and does not address other departments or approved uses of AI.","You generally cannot pen test a public vendor without permission, and it would not stop staff from sharing data.",null,"Password length does nothing to prevent employees from pasting sensitive data into an external service."],2],["sx18",0,1,"An internal chatbot that summarizes support tickets begins revealing its hidden system instructions after a customer submits a ticket containing text that tells the model to ignore previous directions. Which risk has occurred?",["Model poisoning during training","SQL injection","Indirect prompt injection","Model inversion"],2,"Instructions hidden in untrusted content that the model processes (the ticket) override the intended behavior. That is indirect prompt injection; mitigations include isolating untrusted input, limiting tool permissions and filtering outputs.","Domain 1: AI adoption",["Model poisoning corrupts the training data or model weights; here the model was manipulated at run time by input text.","SQL injection targets a database query interpreter, not a language model's instructions.",null,"Model inversion tries to reconstruct training data from model outputs; it does not involve overriding instructions."],2],["sx19",0,2,"An e-commerce site runs in one cloud region. Leadership wants the site to keep serving customers if the entire region fails. Which design meets the requirement?",["A multi-region deployment with replicated data and DNS-based failover","Two instances in the same availability zone behind a load balancer with health checks","Nightly backups of the database stored in the same region","Larger instance sizes with more CPU and memory in the current region"],0,"Only geographic dispersion across regions survives a regional outage. Same-zone redundancy, same-region backups and vertical scaling all share the region as a single point of failure.","Domain 2: Resilient design",[null,"Both instances share one availability zone and region, so a regional failure takes them down together.","Backups in the same region are lost or unreachable with the region, and restoring from them is slow.","Vertical scaling adds capacity but leaves the whole site dependent on one region."],2],["sx20",0,2,"During a sudden spike in traffic, a streaming service disables personalized recommendations but keeps video playback working. Which resilience principle is this?",["Graceful degradation","Fail open","Horizontal scaling","Blue-green deployment"],0,"Graceful degradation keeps core functions available by shedding non-essential features under stress.","Domain 2: Resilient design",[null,"Fail open describes a control that allows traffic when it fails, such as a firewall passing everything; it is not about shedding features.","Horizontal scaling adds more instances to handle load; here the service reduced features instead of adding capacity.","Blue-green deployment is a release technique that switches between two environments, unrelated to load shedding."],1],["sx21",0,2,"An attacker who compromises one web server should not be able to reach other web servers in the same subnet. Which control addresses this east-west risk most directly?",["Microsegmentation with workload-level policies","A perimeter firewall at the internet edge","A larger subnet mask for the web tier","A web application firewall in front of the site"],0,"Microsegmentation enforces policy between individual workloads, even inside one subnet, limiting lateral movement.","Domain 2: Network architecture",[null,"The perimeter firewall inspects north-south traffic and does not see traffic between servers in the same subnet.","Changing the subnet mask only changes addressing; hosts in the same subnet still reach each other.","A WAF filters inbound HTTP requests to the application, not server-to-server traffic inside the network."],2],["sx22",0,2,"A hospital needs to ensure that only corporate devices with current patches and running EDR can join the wired network, while noncompliant devices land in a remediation VLAN. Which solution fits?",["MAC address filtering on each switch","A DMZ for all hospital workstations","Network access control with 802.1X and posture assessment","Static IP addressing for managed devices"],2,"NAC authenticates devices with 802.1X, checks posture (patches, EDR) and assigns VLANs dynamically, including a remediation VLAN.","Domain 2: Network architecture",["MAC filtering is easily spoofed and cannot check patch level or EDR status.","A DMZ (screened subnet) is for public-facing services, not for placing internal workstations.",null,"Static addresses do not verify device identity or health and are trivial to copy."],2],["sx23",0,2,"In a zero trust architecture, which component evaluates identity, device health and context to decide whether a request should be allowed?",["The policy enforcement point","The policy decision point (policy engine)","The certificate revocation list","The jump server"],1,"In the NIST SP 800-207 model the policy engine and policy administrator make up the policy decision point that decides; the policy enforcement point carries out the decision in front of the resource.","Domain 2: Zero trust",["The enforcement point sits in the data path and applies the decision, but it does not make it.",null,"A CRL lists revoked certificates; it can be one input to trust decisions but does not evaluate requests.","A jump server is an administrative access host, not the component that evaluates zero trust policy."],1],["sx24",0,2,"A company moving to zero trust wants remote users to reach only the specific internal apps they are authorized for, without placing them on the internal network. What should replace the full-tunnel VPN?",["A split-tunnel VPN into the same flat network","Port forwarding each application on the edge firewall","A shared administrator account for remote access","Zero trust network access (ZTNA) brokered per application"],3,"ZTNA grants access to individual applications after identity and device checks, without network-level access, reducing lateral movement.","Domain 2: Zero trust",["Split tunneling only changes which traffic goes through the VPN; users still land on the internal network.","Exposing each app directly to the internet enlarges the attack surface and gives no per-user verification.","Shared accounts remove individual accountability and violate least privilege.",null],3],["sx25",0,2,"A development team wants to catch hard-coded credentials and injection flaws in code before it is merged, without running the application. Which testing type fits?",["Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Static application security testing (SAST)","Fuzzing the production API","A red team engagement"],1,"SAST analyzes source code or binaries without executing them, so it can run on every pull request.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["DAST requires a running application and tests it from the outside, usually later in the pipeline.",null,"Fuzzing production is risky and requires a running target; it also would not examine code before merge.","A red team tests the organization's detection and response, not each code change before merge."],1],["sx26",0,2,"A build pipeline pulls dozens of open-source packages. Security wants to be alerted to known vulnerable versions and license conflicts. Which tool category addresses this?",["Interactive application security testing (IAST)","Software composition analysis (SCA)","Runtime application self-protection (RASP)","Host-based intrusion detection (HIDS)"],1,"SCA inventories third-party components, matches them against vulnerability databases and flags license issues, and can produce an SBOM.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["IAST instruments a running app during tests to find flaws in its behavior; it is not focused on dependency inventories and licenses.",null,"RASP protects an application at run time by blocking attacks; it does not report vulnerable dependency versions in builds.","A HIDS monitors a host for suspicious activity and file changes, not package versions in a build."],1],["sx27",0,2,"Attackers modified a build server script so that signed releases contained a backdoor. Which control most directly reduces this CI/CD risk?",["Running a full DAST scan against every signed release after it has been deployed to production servers","Adding a longer password to the source repository","Moving the build server into the DMZ","Isolated, hardened build runners with signed build provenance and reviewed pipeline changes"],3,"Protecting the integrity of the build itself (isolated ephemeral runners, reviewed pipeline changes, provenance attestations) prevents tampering that signing alone cannot catch, because the backdoor was signed legitimately.","Domain 2: Secure SDLC",["DAST tests application behavior from outside and is unlikely to detect a stealthy backdoor inserted at build time.","A stronger repository password does not protect the build server that was actually tampered with.","Placing the build server in a DMZ exposes it more, rather than isolating and protecting it.",null],3],["sx28",0,2,"A WAF in blocking mode is rejecting legitimate customer checkout requests that contain apostrophes in street addresses. What is the best way to resolve it?",["Add a narrow exception for that field to the specific rule and keep blocking mode","Switch the WAF to detection-only mode permanently","Remove the WAF and rely on the network firewall","Disable the entire SQL injection rule group on the checkout application and its APIs"],0,"Troubleshooting controls means fixing the false positive precisely, such as excluding one parameter from one rule, while keeping protection for everything else.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",[null,"Permanent detection-only mode stops blocking real attacks everywhere to fix one field.","A network firewall cannot inspect HTTP payloads, so removing the WAF drops application-layer protection.","Disabling the whole rule group removes SQL injection protection from every page, not just the address field."],1],["sx29",0,2,"The SOC cannot correlate firewall and server logs because events appear out of order by several minutes. What should be fixed first?",["Synchronize every log source to a common NTP time source","Increase the SIEM license so it can accept more events per second","Convert all logs to CSV before ingestion","Shorten the log retention period"],0,"Correlation depends on accurate timestamps. Time drift between sources makes events appear out of order; NTP synchronization (and consistent time zones) fixes it.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",[null,"More licensed capacity does not change the timestamps that are wrong at the source.","Changing the file format does not correct clock drift on the devices.","Retention controls how long logs are kept and has nothing to do with ordering."],1],["sx30",0,2,"A proxy deployed for TLS inspection breaks a banking application that uses certificate pinning. What is the most appropriate fix?",["Disable certificate pinning on every client device","Add a bypass for that application's destinations in the inspection policy","Install the bank's private key on the proxy","Turn off TLS inspection for all outbound traffic across the organization"],1,"Pinned apps reject the proxy's substitute certificate by design. A targeted inspection bypass for trusted destinations is the standard fix, keeping inspection elsewhere.","Domain 2: Integrating controls",["Pinning is set by the app developer and cannot generally be disabled by the customer; weakening it would also reduce security.",null,"You cannot obtain a third party's private key, and doing so would be a serious compromise of their security.","Turning off all inspection gives up visibility across the whole organization to fix one app."],1],["sx31",0,2,"A company wants to stop employees from emailing documents labeled Confidential to personal addresses, and to log attempts. Which control fits best?",["Data loss prevention policies that use classification labels","Full-disk encryption on laptops","Network address translation at the edge","An IDS signature for SMTP traffic"],0,"DLP inspects content and labels in motion and can block, quarantine or log sending of classified data to unapproved destinations.","Domain 2: Data security",[null,"Full-disk encryption protects data at rest on a lost device, not data an authorized user sends by email.","NAT translates addresses and has no awareness of document labels.","An IDS signature looks for attack patterns, not for sensitive business content leaving by email."],1],["sx32",0,2,"A payment system must let a fraud-analytics platform correlate transactions by card number without that platform ever storing real card numbers. Which technique fits?",["Static data masking of the card numbers","Hashing card numbers with MD5","Base64 encoding of the card numbers","Tokenization with a secured token vault"],3,"Tokens are consistent substitutes, so analytics can correlate by token, and only the vault can map back to the real number, reducing PCI DSS scope for the analytics platform.","Domain 2: Data security",["Static masking typically replaces or hides digits irreversibly, which can break correlation and cannot be reversed for authorized use.","Unsalted MD5 of a card number is weak because the card number space is small enough to brute-force.","Base64 is encoding, not protection; anyone can decode it to the real card number.",null],3],["sx33",0,2,"A company wants employees to sign in once with corporate credentials to several SaaS apps, with the identity provider sending signed XML assertions. Which protocol is being used?",["OAuth 2.0","Kerberos","RADIUS","SAML 2.0"],3,"SAML 2.0 exchanges signed XML assertions between an identity provider and service providers for web SSO.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["OAuth 2.0 issues access tokens for delegated authorization and does not use XML assertions for SSO.","Kerberos uses tickets from a KDC inside a domain, not XML assertions to SaaS providers.","RADIUS provides AAA for network access such as VPN and Wi-Fi, not browser-based SaaS SSO.",null],2],["sx34",0,2,"A mobile app needs to call a user's calendar API on their behalf without ever handling the user's password. Which approach is correct?",["Storing the user's password in the app and sending it with each call","OAuth 2.0 implicit flow with long-lived tokens","OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE","Sharing a single API key embedded in the app"],2,"OAuth 2.0 delegates scoped access with tokens. For public clients like mobile apps, the authorization code flow with PKCE is the recommended pattern; the implicit flow is deprecated in current guidance.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Storing and replaying passwords is exactly what delegated authorization exists to avoid, and it exposes the credential.","The implicit flow returns tokens in the browser redirect and is no longer recommended; long-lived tokens make it worse.",null,"A shared embedded key can be extracted from the app and does not identify or limit access per user."],2],["sx35",0,2,"Administrators currently hold permanent Domain Admin rights. Leadership wants them to request elevation for a limited time with approval and session recording. Which solution fits?",["A longer password policy for administrators","A single shared admin account with a vaulted password","Privileged access management with just-in-time elevation","Role-based access with permanent group membership"],2,"PAM with just-in-time access grants privileges only when needed, for a limited time, with approval workflows and session recording, removing standing privilege.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Longer passwords do not remove standing privileges or add approval and recording.","A shared account removes individual accountability even if its password is vaulted.",null,"Permanent group membership is the standing privilege leadership wants to remove."],2],["sx36",0,2,"Conditional access should block sign-ins from unmanaged devices to finance apps but allow them to the intranet with MFA. Which inputs are the policy using?",["Only the user's password strength","Device compliance state, application sensitivity and user identity","Only the source IP address","The user's job title and office phone number"],1,"Conditional access evaluates signals such as identity, device health or management, application and risk to decide whether to allow, require MFA or block.","Domain 2: Identity architecture",["Password strength is not evaluated at sign-in and does not tell the policy whether the device is managed.",null,"Source IP alone cannot distinguish managed from unmanaged devices.","Job title and phone number are directory attributes, not the device and app signals the policy described relies on."],2],["sx37",0,2,"In an IaaS deployment, who is responsible for patching the guest operating system on a virtual machine?",["The customer","The cloud provider","The hypervisor vendor","Shared equally, depending on the region"],0,"Under the shared responsibility model, IaaS customers manage the guest OS, applications, data and identity configuration; the provider secures the physical hosts and hypervisor.","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",[null,"The provider patches physical infrastructure and the hypervisor, not the customer's guest OS in IaaS.","The hypervisor vendor maintains the virtualization layer and has no role in patching customer guest operating systems.","Responsibility splits by service model, not by region, and guest OS patching is the customer's in IaaS."],1],["sx38",0,2,"A cloud team discovers storage buckets set to public and security groups open to 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH across many accounts. Which tool continuously finds and reports these misconfigurations?",["Cloud security posture management (CSPM)","A cloud access security broker in forward-proxy mode","A web application firewall","A hardware security module"],0,"CSPM continuously checks cloud configurations against benchmarks and policies and flags risky settings such as public buckets and open management ports.","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",[null,"A forward-proxy CASB governs user access to SaaS, not the configuration of IaaS resources in your accounts.","A WAF filters HTTP requests to applications and cannot see bucket permissions or security group rules.","An HSM stores and uses cryptographic keys and does not scan cloud configurations."],2],["sx39",0,2,"A company wants cloud-delivered secure web gateway, CASB, ZTNA and firewall services combined with SD-WAN for its branch offices. Which architecture is this?",["A screened subnet","A hub-and-spoke MPLS WAN","Secure access service edge (SASE)","A virtual desktop infrastructure"],2,"SASE converges networking (SD-WAN) with cloud-delivered security services (SSE: SWG, CASB, ZTNA, FWaaS).","Domain 2: Cloud architecture",["A screened subnet is a network zone for public-facing services, not a cloud-delivered security service bundle.","MPLS hub and spoke is a WAN topology that backhauls traffic; it does not include the listed security services.",null,"VDI hosts desktops centrally and does not provide SD-WAN or web security services."],2],["sx40",0,2,"A container image in production is found to contain an AWS access key in an environment file. What is the best long-term fix?",["Base64-encode the key in the Dockerfile","Move the key into a different layer of the image","Make the container registry private and restrict who can pull the image","Inject secrets at run time from a secrets manager and scan images in CI"],3,"Secrets should never be baked into images. Pull them at run time from a secrets manager, rotate the exposed key and add secret scanning to the build.","Domain 2: Containers",["Base64 is reversible encoding and hides nothing from anyone who pulls the image.","Every layer is part of the image and can be extracted, so moving it to another layer does not remove it.","A private registry limits who can pull images, but anyone with pull access still gets the key.",null],3],["sx41",0,2,"A Kubernetes cluster runs workloads from several teams. Which measure best limits the damage if one pod is compromised?",["Giving every pod cluster-admin rights for easier troubleshooting","Running all workloads in the default namespace","Exposing the API server publicly for convenience","Network policies, least-privilege service accounts and blocking privileged containers"],3,"Restricting pod-to-pod traffic, limiting RBAC for service accounts and enforcing pod security standards (no privileged containers) contain a compromised pod.","Domain 2: Containers",["cluster-admin on every pod gives an attacker full control of the cluster from any compromised workload.","Putting all workloads in one namespace removes an isolation and policy boundary between teams.","A publicly exposed API server increases the attack surface of the control plane.",null],3],["sx42",0,2,"A company runs workloads in two public clouds and on premises. Auditors find that each environment has different encryption key policies and rotation schedules. What design change best addresses this?",["Letting each cloud team choose keys independently","Central key management with one consistent policy integrated with each cloud","Disabling encryption in the smaller cloud","Using the same static encryption key in all three environments so the policy is identical"],1,"Centralized key management and policy across environments give consistent rotation, access control and auditing, which is a core multicloud design concern.","Domain 2: Hybrid and multicloud",["Independent choices are what created the inconsistent policies the auditors flagged.",null,"Disabling encryption removes protection and likely violates compliance requirements.","One static shared key has no rotation and turns a single compromise into a breach of every environment."],2],["sx43",0,3,"After a planned certificate renewal on the identity provider, every SAML sign-in to a SaaS app fails with an invalid signature error, while other federated apps work. What is the most likely cause?",["The SaaS app still trusts the old IdP signing certificate","The users' passwords expired at the same time","The SaaS app's DNS records were deleted","The users' MFA authenticator devices lost time synchronization with the IdP"],0,"The service provider validates assertion signatures against the IdP certificate it has stored. When the IdP rotates its signing certificate, each SP must be updated with the new certificate or metadata.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",[null,"Expired passwords would fail at the IdP login step, not produce a signature validation error at one SaaS app.","Missing DNS records would stop the app loading at all rather than returning a signature error.","MFA time drift would fail authentication at the IdP for all apps, not signature validation at one app."],3],["sx44",0,3,"Users report that their TOTP codes from an authenticator app are rejected intermittently on one RADIUS server only. The other RADIUS server works. What should be checked first?",["The users' password complexity","The TLS version on the corporate website","Clock synchronization (NTP) on the failing RADIUS server","The DHCP lease time on the user VLAN"],2,"TOTP codes depend on time. If one validating server's clock drifts, codes fall outside its acceptance window while the other server still accepts them.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",["Password complexity does not affect whether a time-based one-time code is accepted.","The website's TLS version has nothing to do with RADIUS validation of TOTP codes.",null,"DHCP lease times affect addressing on clients, not the validation window on a server."],3],["sx45",0,3,"Certificate-based smart card logons fail for all users after an internal CA's CRL distribution point server was decommissioned. What is happening?",["The users' private keys were erased from their smart cards during the server change","The smart card readers need new firmware","Domain controllers cannot retrieve the CRL, so certificate validation fails","Kerberos was disabled on the domain"],2,"When revocation checking is required and the CRL cannot be retrieved, validation fails closed. Publishing the CRL at a reachable location (or an OCSP responder) restores logons.","Domain 3: Troubleshooting IAM",["Decommissioning a CRL server does not touch keys stored on users' smart cards.","Reader firmware is unrelated to a server being removed and would not break all users at the same moment.",null,"Nothing in the change disabled Kerberos, and smart card logon itself uses Kerberos PKINIT."],3],["sx46",0,3,"A security team wants to ensure that only approved executables can run on point-of-sale terminals, which have a fixed software set. Which control is most effective?",["Signature-based antivirus alone","Application allow listing","A host-based firewall","Disk quotas"],1,"On fixed-function systems an allow list blocks anything not explicitly approved, including new malware that signatures do not know.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",["Signature-based antivirus only catches known malware and misses new or modified samples.",null,"A host firewall controls network connections, not which programs can execute.","Disk quotas limit storage use and do not prevent code from running."],1],["sx47",0,3,"An engineer must harden 200 new Linux servers consistently and prove they match the approved configuration. Which approach is best?",["Apply a CIS Benchmark baseline with configuration management and scan for drift","Harden each server by hand using a printed checklist signed off by a second engineer","Rely on the vendor's default installation settings","Disable logging to reduce the attack surface"],0,"Codifying a benchmark-based baseline in configuration management applies it consistently, and regular compliance scans detect drift.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",[null,"Manual hardening of 200 servers is slow, error-prone and hard to prove consistent.","Default settings favor compatibility over security and usually leave unnecessary services enabled.","Disabling logging removes detection and audit evidence; it does not meaningfully reduce the attack surface."],1],["sx48",0,3,"EDR alerts show that an office document spawned PowerShell, which then made an outbound connection. What capability allowed EDR to detect this when antivirus did not?",["File hash matching against known malware","Full-disk encryption","Scheduled vulnerability scanning","Behavioral monitoring of process relationships and actions"],3,"EDR records process trees, command lines and network activity, so it can flag suspicious behavior such as an Office app launching a script interpreter, even without a known signature.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",["Hash matching only identifies files already known as malicious; the behavior here involves legitimate binaries.","Full-disk encryption protects data at rest and provides no visibility into running processes.","Vulnerability scanners find missing patches and misconfigurations, not live malicious behavior.",null],2],["sx49",0,3,"A company wants laptops to prove to a network access server that their boot chain has not been modified before they get network access. Which technology provides the evidence?",["BIOS password","Full-disk encryption with a passphrase only","Measured boot with TPM-based remote attestation","UEFI legacy compatibility mode"],2,"Measured boot records hashes of each boot component in TPM PCRs; remote attestation sends a signed quote of those measurements to a verifier.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["A BIOS password restricts firmware settings but produces no evidence of boot integrity.","A passphrase-only disk encryption setup protects data but does not measure or attest to the boot chain.",null,"Legacy compatibility mode disables Secure Boot protections, weakening boot integrity rather than proving it."],2],["sx50",0,3,"A payment processor needs tamper-resistant hardware to generate and use thousands of keys for transaction signing, with FIPS 140 validation. Which should it deploy?",["Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","Self-encrypting drives","A software key store on the application server","Hardware security module (HSM)"],3,"HSMs are dedicated, tamper-resistant devices (or cloud services) built for high-volume key generation and cryptographic operations, commonly FIPS 140 validated.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["A TPM is a per-device chip designed for platform keys and attestation, not high-volume transaction signing for many applications.","Self-encrypting drives protect data on that disk and do not provide a signing service.","A software key store keeps keys in server memory and files, which lacks tamper resistance.",null],3],["sx51",0,3,"Secure Boot prevents a server from loading a modified bootloader. How does it do this?",["The TPM encrypts the bootloader with a random key","The OS antivirus scans the bootloader after boot","The firmware compares the bootloader's file size and timestamp with values stored in NVRAM","Firmware verifies the bootloader's signature against trusted keys before running it"],3,"Secure Boot checks each boot component's signature against keys in the firmware databases and refuses to run unsigned or untrusted code. Measured boot, by contrast, records measurements rather than blocking.","Domain 3: Hardware security",["The TPM stores measurements and keys; it does not encrypt the bootloader as part of Secure Boot.","Antivirus runs after the OS loads, which is too late to stop a malicious bootloader.","File size and timestamp checks are trivial to spoof; Secure Boot uses cryptographic signatures.",null],2],["sx52",0,3,"A water utility's PLCs run an unsupported OS that the vendor will not let the utility patch. Which is the best compensating control strategy?",["Install the latest desktop antivirus on each PLC","Connect the PLCs directly to the corporate network so central IT tools can manage them","Reboot the PLCs weekly to clear any malware","Place them in a restricted OT zone with monitored remote access and passive monitoring"],3,"When patching is not possible, isolate and monitor: zones and conduits (as in ISA/IEC 62443 and the Purdue model), controlled remote access and passive OT monitoring that does not disrupt operations.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",["PLCs usually cannot run desktop antivirus, and installing unapproved software can void vendor support or disrupt control.","Connecting PLCs to the corporate network greatly expands exposure to IT-borne threats.","Frequent reboots threaten availability and safety, and persistent threats can survive them.",null],3],["sx53",0,3,"A factory must send sensor data from its OT network to corporate analytics while guaranteeing that nothing can be sent back into the OT network. Which technology fits?",["A bidirectional site-to-site VPN","A jump box with RDP","A unidirectional gateway (data diode)","A shared VLAN trunk between OT and IT"],2,"A data diode physically allows traffic in one direction only, so data can leave the OT network while inbound paths do not exist.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",["A VPN carries traffic both ways, so it cannot guarantee that nothing enters the OT network.","A jump box provides interactive access into the network, which is the opposite of the requirement.",null,"Trunking VLANs between OT and IT creates a two-way path and weakens segmentation."],2],["sx54",0,3,"In industrial control systems, which security objective usually takes top priority?",["Availability and safety of the physical process","Confidentiality of engineering documents","Anonymity of operators","Non-repudiation of emails"],0,"OT environments prioritize availability and safety because downtime or unexpected behavior can harm people, equipment and the environment. This is why changes are cautious and scans are carefully controlled.","Domain 3: Specialized systems",[null,"Confidentiality matters, but in OT it usually ranks below keeping the physical process running safely.","Operator anonymity is not a goal; accountability for control actions is usually wanted.","Email non-repudiation is a general IT concern and not the top priority of control systems."],1],["sx55",0,3,"A security team keeps finding servers where engineers changed firewall rules by hand, making them differ from the Terraform definitions. What is this called, and how should it be handled?",["Configuration drift; detect it with scans and reapply the code-defined state","Privilege escalation; remove the engineers' accounts and reset every admin password","Shadow IT; buy a CASB","Race condition; add locking to the application"],0,"Drift is when real configuration diverges from the declared infrastructure as code. Regular plan runs or compliance scans detect it, and changes should go through code review and the pipeline.","Domain 3: Automation",[null,"Privilege escalation is gaining rights beyond those granted; these engineers used access they had.","Shadow IT is unsanctioned services outside IT's control; a CASB would not reconcile Terraform state.","A race condition is a timing flaw in code, not a mismatch between declared and actual configuration."],2],["sx56",0,3,"A SOC wants phishing reports to be triaged automatically: extract URLs, check reputation, quarantine matching emails across mailboxes and open a ticket. Which technology fits?",["A vulnerability scanner","A SOAR playbook","A network TAP","A DLP endpoint agent"],1,"Security orchestration, automation and response tools run playbooks that call multiple tools' APIs, reducing repetitive analyst work.","Domain 3: Automation",["A vulnerability scanner identifies weaknesses on hosts and cannot orchestrate email triage.",null,"A TAP copies network traffic for monitoring and performs no actions.","A DLP agent watches data leaving endpoints and does not triage reported emails."],1],["sx57",0,3,"An engineer writes a Python script to disable accounts listed in a CSV. Before running it in production, what is the most important safeguard?",["Run it with domain admin rights to avoid errors","Test it in a non-production environment with a dry-run mode and peer review","Schedule it to run during peak business hours","Remove all logging to improve performance"],1,"Automation can cause widespread outages quickly. Testing, dry runs, code review and least-privilege service accounts reduce that risk.","Domain 3: Automation",["Excessive privilege increases the damage if the script has a bug or the CSV is wrong.",null,"Running during peak hours maximizes the impact of any mistake.","Logging is needed to audit what the script changed and to recover from errors."],1],["sx58",0,3,"A government agency is concerned that encrypted traffic captured today could be decrypted years from now. Which planning step addresses this concern?",["Switch from AES-256 to 3DES","Inventory cryptography and plan migration to post-quantum algorithms such as ML-KEM","Increase all RSA key sizes to 4096 bits and treat that as the complete long-term fix","Use MD5 for integrity checks"],1,"This is the harvest-now, decrypt-later threat. Quantum computers would break RSA and elliptic curve key exchange, so agencies build crypto agility and migrate to NIST post-quantum standards such as ML-KEM (FIPS 203).","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["3DES is a deprecated, weaker cipher, and symmetric algorithms were not the main quantum concern anyway.",null,"Bigger RSA keys do not provide lasting protection against a large quantum computer running Shor's algorithm.","MD5 is broken for collision resistance and does nothing about future decryption of traffic."],2],["sx59",0,3,"A web application must store user passwords so that offline cracking of a stolen database is slow. Which approach is best?",["SHA-256 with no salt","AES-256 encryption of each password with the key stored in the same database","A salted, deliberately slow hash such as Argon2id, bcrypt or PBKDF2","A reversible Base64 encoding"],2,"Key stretching functions are deliberately slow and use unique salts, so precomputed tables fail and each guess costs significant compute.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Unsalted fast hashes are vulnerable to rainbow tables and extremely fast GPU cracking.","Encryption is reversible, and keeping the key in the same database gives an attacker everything at once.",null,"Base64 is an encoding that anyone can reverse instantly."],1],["sx60",0,3,"An auditor asks how the company ensures that a stolen server private key cannot decrypt previously captured TLS sessions. What is the correct answer?",["The servers use ephemeral (EC)DHE key exchange for forward secrecy","The servers use RSA key transport to protect each session key with the certificate key","The certificates have a 10-year validity period","The servers compress data before encrypting it"],0,"With ephemeral Diffie-Hellman, each session key is derived from temporary values that are discarded, so the long-term key alone cannot recover past sessions. TLS 1.3 uses only forward-secret key exchange.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",[null,"With RSA key transport, the session key is encrypted with the server's long-term key, so stealing it decrypts recorded sessions.","A long validity period increases exposure and has nothing to do with protecting past sessions.","Compression before encryption can even leak information (as in CRIME) and does not provide forward secrecy."],3],["sx61",0,3,"A research firm wants a cloud provider to compute statistics on encrypted data without ever decrypting it. Which technique fits?",["Envelope encryption","Tokenization","Homomorphic encryption","Steganography"],2,"Homomorphic encryption allows certain computations on ciphertext, with results that decrypt to the same answer as computing on plaintext. It is computationally expensive but protects data in use.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Envelope encryption wraps data keys with a master key; data must still be decrypted to compute on it.","Tokenization substitutes values with tokens, and statistics cannot be computed on the underlying values.",null,"Steganography hides the existence of data within other data; it does not enable computation on it."],2],["sx62",0,3,"A cloud application encrypts each file with its own data key, and that data key is encrypted by a master key held in the provider's KMS. What is this pattern called?",["Key escrow","Envelope encryption","Perfect forward secrecy","Key stretching"],1,"Envelope encryption uses data encryption keys for bulk data, wrapped by a key encryption key in a KMS or HSM. Rotating the master key only requires re-wrapping data keys.","Domain 3: Advanced cryptography",["Key escrow stores a copy of keys with a trusted party for recovery, which is a different concept.",null,"Forward secrecy concerns session keys in key exchange protocols, not wrapping file keys.","Key stretching slows password-derived keys; it does not describe wrapping data keys with a master key."],3],["sx63",0,3,"A software vendor wants customers to verify that an installer came from the vendor and was not altered after release. Which control provides this?",["Encrypting the installer with AES","Publishing the installer over HTTP","Code signing with a certificate from a trusted CA","Compressing the installer into a ZIP file"],2,"A digital signature over the code proves the publisher's identity and integrity; the OS verifies the signature and certificate chain before running it.","Domain 3: Cryptographic use cases",["Encryption provides confidentiality but, on its own, does not prove who published the file.","Plain HTTP allows modification in transit and provides no origin proof.",null,"Compression changes the size of the file and gives no integrity or authenticity assurance."],2],["sx64",0,3,"Two microservices must authenticate each other on every API call, even inside the private network. Which control fits?",["Server-only TLS with a public CA certificate","Mutual TLS with certificates issued to each service","IP allow lists only","HTTP basic authentication over plain HTTP"],1,"In mutual TLS both client and server present certificates, so each proves its identity, and traffic is encrypted. Service meshes often automate this.","Domain 3: PKI",["Server-only TLS authenticates the server to the client but not the calling service.",null,"IP allow lists can be bypassed from a compromised host inside the range and do not prove service identity.","Basic auth over plain HTTP sends credentials in clear text on every call."],2],["sx65",0,3,"A company's issuing CA was compromised. Which PKI design choice limits the damage most?",["A single online root CA that issues all certificates","Certificates with no expiration date","Disabling revocation checking on clients to reduce load on the issuing CA","An offline root CA with separate online intermediate issuing CAs"],3,"With an offline root, the organization can revoke the compromised intermediate and issue a new one without rebuilding trust from the root.","Domain 3: PKI",["If the only CA is an online root and it is compromised, the entire trust hierarchy must be rebuilt.","Non-expiring certificates stay valid forever, making compromises last longer.","Without revocation checking, certificates from the compromised CA cannot be effectively distrusted.",null],3],["sx66",0,3,"Clients checking certificate revocation are slow because they download a large CRL. The team also wants to avoid each client contacting the CA directly. Which solution fits?",["Increasing the CRL publication interval to one month","Removing the CRL distribution point from certificates","OCSP stapling on the web servers","Using self-signed certificates on each server"],2,"With OCSP stapling, the server fetches a signed, time-stamped OCSP response and presents it in the TLS handshake, so clients get fresh status without downloading CRLs or contacting the CA.","Domain 3: PKI",["Publishing less often makes revocation information staler without making it smaller or faster.","Removing the CDP stops clients from checking revocation at all.",null,"Self-signed certificates break the trust chain and have no revocation infrastructure."],2],["sx67",0,3,"A company's domain is being spoofed in phishing emails. It has SPF and DKIM in place. Which DNS record tells receiving servers to reject messages that fail alignment and send reports?",["A DMARC record with p=reject","An MX record with a lower preference","A PTR record for the mail server","A CNAME for the domain's website"],0,"DMARC builds on SPF and DKIM, checks alignment with the visible From domain and publishes a policy (none, quarantine or reject) plus reporting addresses. Organizations usually move from p=none to p=reject in stages.","Domain 3: Email and DNS security",[null,"MX records direct inbound mail delivery; their preference values do not tell receivers to reject spoofed mail.","A PTR record maps an IP to a name for reverse lookups; it does not publish a spoofing policy.","A CNAME aliases one hostname to another and has no role in email authentication."],1],["sx68",0,3,"An organization wants resolvers to be able to verify that DNS answers for its zone are authentic and unmodified. What should it deploy?",["DNS over HTTPS on client browsers","DNSSEC signing of the zone","A longer TTL on all records","Split-horizon DNS"],1,"DNSSEC adds digital signatures to DNS records, and validating resolvers verify them through a chain of trust, preventing forged answers such as cache poisoning.","Domain 3: Email and DNS security",["DoH encrypts the query path between client and resolver but does not prove the zone data is authentic.",null,"A longer TTL affects caching duration, not authenticity.","Split-horizon returns different answers to internal and external clients; it provides no signatures."],2],["sx69",0,3,"Employees use personal phones for corporate email. The company wants to wipe only corporate data if an employee leaves, without touching personal photos. Which capability fits?",["MDM/MAM containerization with selective wipe","Full device wipe of every phone","Disabling the phone camera","Requiring a simple four-digit PIN"],0,"Containerization separates corporate apps and data from personal content, so a selective (enterprise) wipe removes only the corporate container.","Domain 3: Mobile management",[null,"A full wipe would erase personal data, which is not acceptable for BYOD.","Disabling the camera limits a feature but does nothing to remove corporate data.","A simple PIN protects the lock screen but does not separate or remove corporate data."],1],["sx70",0,3,"A network team manages routers with SNMPv2c and community string public. Which change most improves the security of monitoring?",["Change the community string to private","Move SNMP to TCP port 443","Enable SNMP write access for faster changes","Migrate to SNMPv3 with authentication and privacy (authPriv)"],3,"SNMPv3 in authPriv mode adds user-based authentication and encryption; v2c community strings are sent in clear text.","Domain 3: Network infrastructure",["private is also a well-known default and still travels in clear text with v2c.","Changing the port does not add authentication or encryption.","Write access lets anyone with the string change device configuration, increasing risk.",null],1],["sx71",0,4,"A SIEM rule fires hundreds of times a day for failed logins from a vulnerability scanner's IP address during authorized scans. What is the best tuning action?",["Disable the failed-login rule entirely","Delete the scanner's logs before ingestion","Raise the alert threshold to 10,000 failed logins per day for every source","Add a narrow, documented exception for the scanner during its scan window"],3,"Precise, documented exceptions remove known-good noise while keeping the rule effective for everyone else.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["Disabling the rule would blind the SOC to real brute-force and password-spraying activity.","Dropping the scanner's logs removes evidence and could hide misuse of the scanner itself.","A huge global threshold would hide genuine attacks from every other source.",null],2],["sx72",0,4,"A SIEM correlation rule should flag a likely account compromise. Which pattern is the strongest signal?",["One failed login followed by a success from the same workstation","A user logging in at 9 a.m. on a weekday","Repeated failures, a success from a new country, then a new mail forwarding rule","A password change through the normal self-service portal after the 90-day expiry reminder"],2,"Correlating several weak signals in sequence (brute force, anomalous success, post-compromise action) yields a high-fidelity alert.","Domain 4: Monitoring",["A single typo followed by a success from the usual workstation is normal user behavior.","Logging in at the start of the workday is expected activity.",null,"Self-service password changes through the approved portal are routine."],3],["sx73",0,4,"Two organizations want to exchange threat intelligence automatically in a standard structured format over an API. Which pair of standards fits?",["SAML for the data format and LDAP for transport","STIX for the data format and TAXII for transport","CVSS for the data format and SNMP for transport","YARA for the data format and Syslog for transport"],1,"STIX (Structured Threat Information Expression) describes indicators, TTPs and relationships; TAXII defines how to exchange STIX over HTTPS.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",["SAML carries authentication assertions and LDAP queries directories; neither is designed for threat intelligence.",null,"CVSS scores vulnerability severity and SNMP manages network devices; neither shares intelligence.","YARA describes file-matching rules and Syslog carries log messages; together they are not a sharing standard."],1],["sx74",0,4,"Why do detections based on adversary TTPs tend to remain useful longer than those based on file hashes or IP addresses?",["TTPs are always published by vendors within minutes of a new campaign starting","Hashes are too long for SIEM storage","IP addresses cannot be logged by firewalls","Behaviors cost attackers more to change than individual indicators do"],3,"Per the Pyramid of Pain, attackers can change hashes, IPs and domains cheaply, but changing tools and techniques costs them much more.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",["TTP intelligence is not always shared quickly, and speed of sharing is not why it lasts.","Hashes are small and easily stored; the issue is that attackers can change them easily.","Firewalls routinely log IP addresses; the weakness is that attackers rotate them.",null],2],["sx75",0,4,"A threat hunter reads a report that an adversary group uses scheduled tasks for persistence and decides to search endpoint telemetry for unusual task creation. What type of hunt is this?",["Automated signature scanning","Hypothesis-driven, intelligence-based hunting","A vulnerability assessment","A compliance audit"],1,"The hunt starts from a testable hypothesis informed by threat intelligence, then searches data for evidence. Findings should become automated detections.","Domain 4: Threat hunting",["Signature scanning is automated matching of known patterns, not an analyst-led search from a hypothesis.",null,"Vulnerability assessments look for weaknesses, not evidence of attacker activity.","Compliance audits check controls against requirements, not for signs of compromise."],2],["sx76",0,4,"A UEBA system flags an engineer who normally downloads 50 MB per day from the code repository but downloaded 40 GB the day after giving notice. What is the best next step?",["Investigate the activity under the insider threat procedure with HR and legal","Ignore it, because the engineer has legitimate repository access","Immediately post the engineer's name and the download size in a company-wide alert","Delete the code repository to protect the data"],0,"UEBA surfaces deviations from a user's baseline. Legitimate access does not rule out misuse, so analysts investigate and involve HR and legal per policy.","Domain 4: Threat hunting",[null,"Authorized access can still be misused, and a huge spike after resignation is a classic exfiltration pattern.","Publicly naming someone before investigation is unfair and can create legal exposure.","Deleting the repository destroys business assets and evidence without addressing the behavior."],2],["sx77",0,4,"A scanner reports a CVSS 9.8 vulnerability on an isolated lab server and a CVSS 7.5 vulnerability that is listed in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on an internet-facing VPN appliance. Which should be fixed first?",["The lab server, because its CVSS base score of 9.8 is higher than the other finding's","Both at the next quarterly patch window","Neither, until a penetration test confirms them","The VPN appliance, because it is exposed and the flaw is known to be exploited"],3,"Prioritization combines severity with exploitation evidence (KEV, EPSS), exposure and asset criticality. An actively exploited flaw on an internet-facing system is the more urgent risk.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["The CVSS base score alone ignores that the lab server is isolated and the other flaw is being exploited in the wild.","Waiting for a quarterly window leaves an actively exploited, internet-facing flaw open for too long.","Confirming a known exploited flaw with a pen test first only delays remediation.",null],3],["sx78",0,4,"A vulnerability scanner reports an outdated OpenSSL version on a Linux server, but the distribution has backported the security fix into its patched package. How should this finding be handled?",["Compile the newest OpenSSL from source, bypassing the distribution's package manager","Verify the package changelog or advisory, then document it as a false positive","Remove OpenSSL from the server","Accept the risk without checking"],1,"Distributions often backport fixes without changing the upstream version number, which fools version-based checks. Verify with the vendor advisory and record the exception.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["Building from source breaks vendor support and future updates for a problem that may already be fixed.",null,"Many system components depend on OpenSSL, so removing it would break the server.","Accepting without verification might leave a real vulnerability in place."],3],["sx79",0,4,"A web page builds a database query by concatenating user input. What is the most effective fix?",["Hide error messages from users","Block the word SELECT in input fields","Use parameterized queries (prepared statements)","Move the database to a different port"],2,"Parameterized queries keep code and data separate, so input is never interpreted as SQL. Input validation and least-privilege database accounts add defense in depth.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["Hiding errors makes attacks harder to observe but does not remove the injection flaw.","Keyword blocklists are easy to evade and can block legitimate input.",null,"Changing the port does not change how the application builds queries."],1],["sx80",0,4,"A code review finds that a Java service accepts serialized objects from clients and reconstructs them without validation. Which vulnerability class does this create, and what is the fix?",["Cross-site scripting; encode output","Race condition; add a mutex","Directory traversal; canonicalize file paths and reject any path containing ../ sequences","Insecure deserialization; accept only data formats or allow-listed types"],3,"Deserializing untrusted objects can trigger dangerous code paths during object reconstruction. Prefer data-only formats like JSON with schema validation, or restrict deserialization to allow-listed classes.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["XSS involves injecting script into web pages viewed by other users, not reconstructing objects on the server.","A race condition is a timing flaw between concurrent operations, not unsafe object reconstruction.","Directory traversal abuses file paths; this code issue is about deserializing untrusted data.",null],3],["sx81",0,4,"An application checks whether a user has enough balance and then deducts it in a separate step. Attackers submit many simultaneous requests and overdraw accounts. Which flaw is this?",["A buffer overflow","Cross-site request forgery","Broken TLS configuration","A race condition (time-of-check to time-of-use)"],3,"The gap between the check and the use lets concurrent requests all pass the check. Atomic transactions or locking fix it.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",["A buffer overflow writes past the end of a memory buffer; here the logic is correct per request but not atomic.","CSRF tricks a user's browser into sending a request; the problem here is concurrent requests racing each other.","TLS configuration affects transport encryption and would not let users overdraw balances.",null],3],["sx82",0,4,"A development team keeps shipping memory-corruption bugs in a C network parser. Which long-term engineering change most reduces this class of flaw?",["Rewrite it in a memory-safe language such as Rust and fuzz it continuously","Turn off compiler warnings to speed up builds","Increase the server's RAM","Run the parser as root so it never fails on permission errors when reading packets"],0,"Memory-safe languages eliminate whole classes of bugs such as buffer overflows and use-after-free. Continuous fuzzing finds remaining parser edge cases.","Domain 4: Vulnerabilities and attacks",[null,"Compiler warnings often point to memory bugs; turning them off hides problems.","More RAM does not prevent out-of-bounds writes or use-after-free errors.","Running as root makes any memory-corruption bug far more damaging."],2],["sx83",0,4,"An analyst wants to learn what a suspicious file does without letting it harm the network. Which approach is safest and still shows its behavior?",["Open it on the analyst's own workstation","Run it in an isolated sandbox and observe its behavior","Email it to colleagues for their opinion","Upload it to a public file-sharing site so other analysts can look"],1,"Dynamic analysis in an isolated sandbox reveals behavior safely. Static analysis (strings, headers, hashes) can come first without executing anything.","Domain 4: Malware analysis",["Running it on a production workstation risks infecting the machine and the network.",null,"Emailing malware spreads it to more people and systems.","Public uploads can leak sensitive data and tip off attackers."],1],["sx84",0,4,"A SOC wants to detect variants of a malware family that change their file hash on every build but share distinctive code and strings. Which tool fits?",["A list of SHA-256 hashes","A firewall rule that blocks port 80","YARA rules that match the shared patterns","A DMARC policy"],2,"YARA rules describe byte patterns, strings and conditions, catching variants that exact hashes miss.","Domain 4: Malware analysis",["Hash lists only match exact files, so each rebuilt variant slips past.","Blocking port 80 would break web traffic and does not identify malware files.",null,"DMARC controls email spoofing and has nothing to do with identifying malware files."],3],["sx85",0,4,"During a ransomware outbreak, the IR team has identified the infected hosts. According to the IR life cycle, what should happen next?",["Hold the lessons-learned meeting","Contain it by isolating affected hosts and disabling compromised accounts","Restore every affected system from last night's backup immediately, without further investigation","Reimage every computer in the company"],1,"After detection and analysis comes containment, which stops the spread. Eradication and recovery follow, and lessons learned come at the end.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Lessons learned belong to post-incident activity, after the incident is resolved.",null,"Restoring before containment and root cause analysis risks reinfection, and backups may themselves be compromised.","Reimaging every computer is disruptive, destroys evidence and is not the next step."],1],["sx86",0,4,"After eradicating malware, a team restores a server to production. What should the recovery phase include?",["Deleting all logs from the incident","Validating the system is clean and patched, then monitoring it closely","Turning off EDR on the restored server","Closing the incident ticket right away, since the malware has already been eradicated"],1,"Recovery restores service safely: verify integrity, confirm the root cause was fixed and watch for recurrence before declaring the incident closed.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Incident logs are evidence and are needed for lessons learned and possible legal action.",null,"EDR provides the monitoring needed to catch reinfection during recovery.","Closing without follow-up skips monitoring and the post-incident review."],1],["sx87",0,4,"Investigators must collect evidence from a running server suspected of hosting in-memory malware. Which should be captured first?",["Archived backup tapes","The server's hard disk image","System memory (RAM)","Paper printouts of the configuration"],2,"Following the order of volatility, memory is lost on shutdown, so it is captured before disk images and archival sources.","Domain 4: Forensics",["Backup tapes are among the least volatile sources and will still be there later.","Disk data survives a reboot and should be imaged after memory is collected.",null,"Printouts do not capture the in-memory malware, and configuration can be exported later."],2],["sx88",0,4,"A forensic image of a laptop will be used in court. What most helps prove it was not altered after collection?",["Storing the image on a shared network drive that the whole IR team can access","Renaming the file with the case number","Compressing the image to save space","Hashing it at acquisition and recording a chain of custody"],3,"A cryptographic hash taken at acquisition can be recomputed later to prove integrity, and chain-of-custody records show who handled the evidence and when.","Domain 4: Forensics",["A shared drive gives broad access and weakens assurance that nobody altered the image.","A file name does not prove anything about whether the contents changed.","Compression changes storage size but does not prove the evidence is unchanged.",null],3],["sx89",0,4,"A company acquired several subsidiaries and suspects there are internet-facing servers nobody knows about. Which activity addresses this first?",["Increasing password length requirements for all subsidiary accounts","Rewriting the acceptable use policy","Buying more SIEM storage","Discovering external domains, IP ranges and exposed services"],3,"You cannot protect assets you do not know about. Attack surface management discovers internet-facing assets so they can be inventoried, owned and secured.","Domain 4: Attack surface management",["Password policy changes do not reveal unknown internet-facing servers.","Policy updates do not locate assets that are missing from the inventory.","More SIEM storage helps only for assets already sending logs, which unknown servers are not.",null],3],["sx90",0,4,"A detection engineer writes a rule in a vendor-neutral YAML format so it can be converted into queries for several different SIEMs. Which format is this?",["Snort","STIX","Sigma","OpenIOC"],2,"Sigma is a generic signature format for log events that tools convert into SIEM-specific query languages.","Domain 4: Detection engineering",["Snort rules inspect network packets for an IDS/IPS, not log events across SIEMs.","STIX is a format for sharing threat intelligence, not writing log detection rules.",null,"OpenIOC is an XML format for describing indicators of compromise, not YAML log detection rules for SIEMs."],1],["x1",0,1,"A legacy clinical system cannot support the encryption standard required by corporate policy, and replacing it will take 18 months. What should the security manager do FIRST?",["Disconnect the system from the network until it is replaced","Rewrite the corporate standard so the legacy system becomes compliant","Let it keep running unchanged, since the replacement project is already approved and funded","File a formal exception with compensating controls and a risk owner's approval"],3,"A documented exception makes the risk visible, time-bound and owned, and compensating controls reduce exposure until replacement. Quietly running out of compliance because a replacement is funded leaves the risk untracked.","Domain 1: Policy exceptions",["Disconnecting a clinical system would halt patient care; the risk should be managed, not solved by an outage.","Weakening the standard for everyone to fit one system lowers protection across the whole organization.","A funded replacement does not reduce today's exposure, and undocumented noncompliance bypasses governance.",null],2],["x2",0,1,"The CISO wants a metric that warns leadership when exposure is increasing before an incident occurs, such as the number of critical vulnerabilities open past their remediation deadline. What type of metric is this?",["Key risk indicator (KRI)","Key performance indicator (KPI)","Recovery time objective (RTO)","Annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)"],0,"A KRI is a leading indicator that signals growing risk exposure, such as overdue critical vulnerabilities. A KPI is tempting but measures process performance, like the share of patches deployed on time, rather than exposure.","Domain 1: Security metrics",[null,"KPIs measure how well a process performs against goals, not whether risk exposure is rising.","RTO is a continuity target for how quickly a process must be restored, not an early warning metric.","ARO estimates how often a loss event happens per year; it is an input to ALE, not a leading indicator."],1],["x3",0,1,"The board rejects a heat map with High, Medium and Low ratings and asks for cyber risk expressed as ranges of probable financial loss over the next year. Which approach BEST meets this request?",["A quantitative model such as FAIR using Monte Carlo simulation","A qualitative assessment with a five-point likelihood and impact scale","A control maturity assessment against a framework","A gap analysis against an industry compliance standard"],0,"FAIR breaks risk into loss event frequency and loss magnitude and, with Monte Carlo simulation, produces probable loss ranges in currency. A finer qualitative scale is still an ordinal rating and cannot express money.","Domain 1: Quantitative risk analysis",[null,"Any ordinal scale, even with five points, still produces ratings, not ranges of financial loss.","Maturity scores show how developed controls are, not how much money the organization is likely to lose.","A gap analysis lists missing requirements; it does not estimate loss magnitude or frequency."],3],["x4",0,1,"Your payroll SaaS provider tells you it uses a subcontractor to host its database. Which contract provision BEST manages the risk this introduces?",["A clause giving your company ownership of the provider's source code","A service level agreement guaranteeing 99.99 percent uptime with credits for every outage","A requirement to disclose subprocessors and flow down equivalent security obligations to them","A non-solicitation clause preventing the provider from hiring your staff"],2,"Fourth-party risk is managed by requiring the vendor to disclose subprocessors and bind them to the same security and audit obligations. An uptime SLA is tempting but only covers availability.","Domain 1: Supply chain risk",["Owning source code, or escrow, addresses vendor failure, not how a subcontractor protects your data.","An uptime SLA covers availability only and says nothing about the subcontractor's security controls.",null,"Non-solicitation protects your workforce, not the confidentiality of data held by a fourth party."],2],["x5",0,1,"A vendor's SOC 2 Type II report covers January through June. It is now late November, and your procurement review needs assurance for the months after June. What should you request from the vendor?",["A SOC 1 Type I report from the vendor describing its control design as of today","The vendor's internal risk register","A new penetration test of your own tenant","A bridge letter covering the period since the report ended"],3,"A bridge (gap) letter is a management assertion that no material control changes occurred between the end of the audited period and a later date, used until the next SOC 2 report. A Type I report would only cover design at a single point.","Domain 1: Third-party assessments",["SOC 1 addresses controls relevant to financial reporting, and a Type I report only describes design at a point in time.","A risk register is an internal tracking tool and does not provide assurance that controls kept operating.","A pen test finds exploitable weaknesses at one moment; it does not attest that the vendor's controls kept operating.",null],3],["x6",0,1,"A retailer wants to reduce the number of systems subject to PCI DSS assessment. Cardholder data is currently reachable from the general office network. Which action MOST reduces scope?",["Deploy antivirus to every office workstation","Increase the frequency of internal and external vulnerability scans across all office networks","Isolate the cardholder data environment with segmentation that blocks other networks","Encrypt the office Wi-Fi network with WPA3"],2,"Systems that can connect to the cardholder data environment are in scope, so effective segmentation shrinks the assessment boundary. Adding controls such as antivirus is useful but does not take any system out of scope.","Domain 1: PCI DSS scope",["Antivirus is a required control, but it does not remove any systems from PCI DSS scope.","Scanning more often improves detection but leaves every connected system in scope.",null,"Stronger Wi-Fi encryption protects the wireless link but does not separate office systems from the CDE."],2],["x7",0,1,"A new customer database is being built. Who should decide its data classification and approve who may access it?",["The database administrator who manages the servers","The security analyst who monitors the SIEM","The data owner, a business leader accountable for the data","The external auditor who reviews the controls"],2,"The data owner is accountable for the data and sets its classification and access rules; custodians such as DBAs implement those decisions. The DBA is tempting because they control the system, but custody is not ownership.","Domain 1: Data roles",["The DBA is a data custodian who implements protections but does not decide classification or access.","Analysts monitor and advise; they are not accountable for the business value or use of the data.",null,"Auditors provide independent assurance and must not make the decisions they later evaluate."],1],["x8",0,1,"A US hospital plans to send patient records to a cloud analytics vendor. Under HIPAA, what must be in place BEFORE any protected health information is shared?",["A SOC 2 Type I report from the vendor","Written consent from every patient in the dataset","A PCI DSS attestation of compliance","A business associate agreement with the vendor"],3,"HIPAA requires covered entities to sign a business associate agreement that obligates the vendor to safeguard PHI. A SOC 2 report is useful evidence but does not satisfy that legal requirement.","Domain 1: HIPAA",["A SOC 2 report may inform due diligence, but HIPAA specifically requires a BAA before sharing PHI.","Sharing PHI with a business associate for permitted operations does not require individual patient consent.","PCI DSS governs payment card data, not health information.",null],1],["x9",0,1,"A critical zero-day is being actively exploited against an internet-facing server, and the next change advisory board meeting is in five days. How should the fix be handled?",["Wait for the scheduled CAB meeting so the normal process is followed","Apply the fix immediately without recording it anywhere, since speed matters more than paperwork","Classify it as a standard pre-approved change","As an emergency change approved by the emergency CAB, then documented and reviewed"],3,"Emergency change procedures allow rapid approval by a smaller emergency CAB, with documentation completed and reviewed afterward. Applying it with no record is tempting but undermines change and configuration management.","Domain 1: Change management",["Waiting five days while exploitation is active accepts serious risk that the emergency process exists to avoid.","Skipping documentation breaks configuration control and leaves no record for rollback or review.","Standard changes are low-risk, routine and pre-approved; an urgent zero-day fix is none of these.",null],2],["x10",0,1,"After a year of monthly phishing simulations, leadership asks for the metric that BEST shows employee behavior has actually improved. Which should the program report?",["The number of employees who completed the annual training module","The total number of simulated emails sent this year","The trend of rising report rates and falling click rates","The percentage of employees who passed the training quiz"],2,"Behavior change shows up as people reporting suspicious mail more often and clicking less over time. Training completion is easy to measure but only proves participation.","Domain 1: Awareness program metrics",["Completion shows attendance, not whether people now recognize and report phishing.","The volume of simulations measures program activity, not employee behavior.",null,"Quiz scores measure short-term knowledge recall rather than real-world behavior."],2],["x11",0,1,"A company that processes EU residents' personal data confirms a breach that is likely to put individuals at risk. Under GDPR, what is the deadline for notifying the supervisory authority?",["Within 30 calendar days after the forensic investigation has been fully completed","Within 24 hours of the first suspicious alert","Without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of awareness","Only after all affected customers have been notified"],2,"GDPR Article 33 requires notifying the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of awareness. Waiting until the investigation ends is tempting but not allowed; details may follow in phases.","Domain 1: Breach notification",["GDPR does not let the controller wait for the investigation to finish; information may be provided in phases.","The GDPR clock starts when the controller becomes aware of a breach, and the limit is 72 hours, not 24.",null,"Authority notification does not wait for individual notices; the two obligations are separate."],2],["x12",0,1,"HR wants to deploy a vendor's AI model that ranks job applicants. Which governance step should happen FIRST?",["Enable the model in production and monitor for complaints","Negotiate a lower license price by sharing applicant data with the vendor","Encrypt the model weights at rest","Perform an AI risk and impact assessment covering bias and human oversight"],3,"Hiring decisions are high impact, so an AI impact assessment should establish fairness testing, transparency and human review before use. Encrypting the model is a valid control but addresses a different risk.","Domain 1: AI governance",["Waiting for complaints means discriminatory outcomes affect real applicants before anyone checks.","Sharing applicant data with the vendor adds privacy risk and does nothing to assess model fairness.","Encryption protects the model's confidentiality but does not address biased or unexplainable decisions.",null],3],["x13",0,2,"A cloud architecture has dozens of VPCs connected through a transit hub. Security requires that all traffic between application VPCs be inspected by a next-generation firewall. Which design BEST achieves this?",["Enable flow logs on every VPC and review them daily","Route inter-VPC traffic through a central inspection VPC that hosts the firewalls","Configure full-mesh VPC peering so every VPC connects directly to all the others, avoiding the hub","Apply the same security group to every instance"],1,"Hub routing that forces east-west traffic through an inspection VPC puts every flow through the firewall. Full-mesh peering is simpler to build but sends traffic around the firewall.","Domain 2: Cloud network design",["Flow logs record metadata after the fact; they do not inspect or block traffic.",null,"Direct peering bypasses any central firewall, which is the opposite of the requirement.","Security groups filter by port and address but do not perform next-generation inspection."],3],["x14",0,2,"A security team wants a high-fidelity alert if an attacker dumps credentials from a server and tries to reuse them. Which deception control BEST provides this?",["Increase password complexity requirements for all accounts","Plant a fake privileged credential on servers and alert on any use of it","Run a separate honeynet on an internet-facing subnet to study attacker behavior","Enable account lockout after five failed attempts"],1,"A honeytoken credential has no legitimate use, so any attempt to use it is a strong indicator of compromise. Account lockout is tempting but does not fire when valid dumped credentials are replayed.","Domain 2: Deception technology",["Stronger passwords make cracking harder but give no alert when stolen credentials are used.",null,"An external honeynet studies attackers; it would not detect credentials stolen from internal servers.","Lockout triggers on guesses, but an attacker replaying a real stolen credential succeeds on the first try."],2],["x15",0,2,"Domain administrators read email and browse the web from the same workstation they use for administration. Which architecture change BEST reduces the risk of their credentials being stolen?",["Give each administrator a second monitor dedicated to administrative consoles","Require dedicated privileged access workstations used only for admin tasks","Allow administration only during business hours","Store admin passwords in a spreadsheet on a network share"],1,"A PAW isolates privileged sessions from high-risk activity such as email and browsing, the most common initial infection paths. Time restrictions sound protective but do not remove the exposure.","Domain 2: Privileged access",["Extra displays change nothing about which software runs in the same session as admin credentials.",null,"Time restrictions do not stop a phishing payload on the same workstation from stealing credentials.","A shared spreadsheet exposes the credentials further and adds a new target for attackers."],1],["x16",0,2,"A company exposes dozens of partner-facing REST APIs, each built by a different team with inconsistent authentication and no throttling. What is the BEST architectural control?",["Move the APIs to a nonstandard TCP port","Require every partner to connect only through a dedicated site-to-site IPsec VPN tunnel","Publish the API documentation only to approved partners","An API gateway that enforces authentication, rate limits and schema validation"],3,"An API gateway centralizes consistent authentication, throttling and request validation for every API. A VPN is tempting but only protects the transport, not the API behavior.","Domain 2: API security",["Changing ports is obscurity and adds no authentication or throttling.","A VPN encrypts the link but still leaves each API with inconsistent authentication and no rate limits.","Hiding documentation does not stop requests to the endpoints themselves.",null],2],["x17",0,2,"A bank wants to process sensitive customer data in a public cloud while ensuring that the cloud operator and hypervisor cannot read the data even while it is being processed. Which technology BEST meets this?",["Confidential computing with hardware TEEs","Customer-managed keys for storage encryption","TLS 1.3 between the application and its users","Dedicated hosts in a single availability zone"],0,"Confidential computing encrypts and isolates data in use inside a hardware TEE, keeping it from the host OS and hypervisor. Customer-managed keys are tempting but only cover data at rest.","Domain 2: Data in use",[null,"Storage keys protect data at rest; data is decrypted in normal memory while it is processed.","TLS protects data in transit, not data sitting in memory on the host.","Dedicated hardware isolates you from other tenants but not from the provider's own hypervisor and operators."],3],["x18",0,2,"An organization wants to find sensitive files that users have already uploaded to its sanctioned SaaS storage and change their sharing settings. Which CASB deployment mode fits BEST?",["Forward proxy on managed endpoints","Reverse proxy in front of the SaaS login page","API-based integration with the SaaS provider","DNS filtering of unsanctioned cloud domains"],2,"API mode connects directly to the SaaS platform, so it can scan existing data at rest and change sharing settings. Proxy modes only see traffic as it passes through them.","Domain 2: CASB",["A forward proxy sees new traffic from managed devices but cannot scan files already stored in the service.","A reverse proxy controls live sessions; it does not reach data at rest that was uploaded earlier.",null,"DNS filtering blocks access to sites but has no view into files stored in a sanctioned service."],2],["x19",0,2,"A load balancer terminates TLS so the WAF can inspect requests, but compliance requires traffic to stay encrypted all the way to the application servers. Which configuration BEST satisfies both needs?",["Use TLS passthrough so the load balancer never decrypts","Re-encrypt traffic from the load balancer to the back ends (TLS bridging)","Terminate TLS and send plain HTTP to back ends on a private subnet","Disable the WAF and rely on server-side input validation"],1,"TLS bridging decrypts for inspection and then re-encrypts to the back end, so every network hop stays encrypted. Passthrough is tempting but blinds the WAF.","Domain 2: TLS inspection design",["Passthrough keeps traffic encrypted but prevents the WAF from inspecting it.",null,"Plain HTTP on the internal hop violates the end-to-end encryption requirement.","Removing the WAF gives up a required inspection layer instead of solving the design conflict."],2],["x20",0,2,"A trading database must not lose any committed transaction if the primary data center fails. The secondary site is 20 km away over low-latency links. Which replication approach meets this?",["Asynchronous replication every five minutes","Synchronous replication that commits on both sites before acknowledging","Nightly full backups that are encrypted and shipped to the secondary site by courier","Hourly snapshots of the primary storage volume"],1,"Synchronous replication gives an RPO of zero because a write is acknowledged only after both sites have it, and 20 km keeps the added latency acceptable. Asynchronous replication is common but can lose recent commits.","Domain 2: Resilience and replication",["Asynchronous replication can lose whatever was committed since the last copy, so RPO is greater than zero.",null,"Nightly backups could lose up to a full day of transactions.","Hourly snapshots still leave up to an hour of committed data at risk."],3],["x21",0,2,"A review finds that all 40 serverless functions in an application share one execution role with full access to every storage bucket and database. What is the BEST improvement?",["Place all of the functions inside a private subnet with no internet gateway","Increase function timeout limits to reduce errors","Give each function its own role limited to the resources it uses","Rotate the shared role's credentials every week"],2,"Per-function least-privilege roles contain the damage if one function is compromised. Rotating credentials sounds protective, but any compromised function would still reach everything.","Domain 2: Serverless security",["Network placement does not narrow what the shared role is allowed to do through the cloud API.","Timeouts affect reliability, not permissions.",null,"Roles already issue short-lived credentials; rotation does not reduce what a compromised function can reach."],2],["x22",0,2,"Engineers keep deploying Terraform that creates unencrypted databases, which the cloud security posture tool flags only after deployment. What is the BEST way to shift this check left?",["Scan the Terraform plan with policy-as-code in the pipeline and block violations","Increase the CSPM scan frequency to every hour","Send engineers a detailed monthly report of all misconfiguration findings and their owners","Give engineers read-only access to the cloud console"],0,"Evaluating infrastructure as code with policy-as-code rules before apply blocks misconfigurations before they exist. Faster CSPM scans are still reactive.","Domain 2: Infrastructure as code",[null,"More frequent scans still find the problem after the database already exists.","Reports inform people after the fact and do not prevent insecure deployments.","Engineers deploy through the pipeline, so removing console rights would not stop the misconfigured Terraform."],1],["x23",0,2,"A team no longer patches running web servers. Instead, every change produces a new hardened image, new instances are deployed from it, and old instances are destroyed. What is this approach called?",["Configuration drift","Hot patching","Snowflake servers","Immutable infrastructure"],3,"Immutable infrastructure replaces instances from a known image instead of modifying them, which prevents drift and simplifies rollback. Configuration drift is a related term but names the problem this solves.","Domain 2: Immutable infrastructure",["Drift is the unwanted divergence of systems from their baseline, which this approach prevents.","Hot patching applies fixes to running systems without a restart, the opposite of replacing them.","Snowflake servers are hand-configured, unique systems, the opposite of servers rebuilt from an image.",null],1],["x24",0,2,"A regulator requires that a cloud provider never be able to access the keys protecting a company's data, even under legal compulsion served on the provider. Which key management model BEST meets this?",["Provider-managed keys with automatic annual rotation","Bring your own key imported into the provider's KMS","Default server-side encryption using the platform's built-in keys and settings","Hold your own key in an external HSM the provider cannot access"],3,"With hold-your-own-key or external key management, the key stays in customer-controlled hardware and the provider must request each use. BYOK is tempting, but the imported key still resides in the provider's service.","Domain 2: Cloud key management",["The provider controls these keys, so it can use them to decrypt data.","Once imported, the key lives in the provider's KMS, which the provider operates.","Default encryption is entirely provider-controlled, including the keys.",null],3],["x25",0,2,"A penetration test shows that a web app's URL-fetching feature can be abused to request the cloud instance metadata service and obtain temporary credentials. Which control BEST blocks this path at the platform level?",["Require token-based metadata access (such as IMDSv2) with a hop limit of 1","Add a WAF rule that blocks requests containing the word metadata anywhere in the query string","Remove the instance role and embed access keys in the code","Enable encryption at rest on the instance's disks"],0,"Token-based metadata access requires a session token obtained with a separate PUT request that a typical SSRF cannot make, and a hop limit of 1 keeps tokens on the host. Fixing URL validation in the app also matters, but this is the platform control.","Domain 2: Cloud workload security",[null,"Keyword filters are easy to evade with encoding or redirects and do not fix the platform exposure.","Embedded long-lived keys are worse and can be stolen from the code or the file system.","Disk encryption does not affect requests the application makes to the metadata endpoint."],3],["x26",0,2,"When employees leave, their accounts in 30 SaaS applications stay active for weeks because IT removes them by hand. Which standard should the identity provider use to automate provisioning and deprovisioning?",["SAML","LDAP referrals","Kerberos","SCIM"],3,"SCIM is a standard REST-based protocol for creating, updating and deleting user accounts in applications from a central identity source. SAML is tempting but only carries authentication assertions, so local accounts can linger.","Domain 2: Identity lifecycle",["SAML carries single sign-on assertions; it does not create or delete accounts in the apps.","LDAP referrals direct directory queries to other servers and do not provision SaaS accounts.","Kerberos authenticates users in a domain but has no role in the SaaS account lifecycle.",null],2],["x27",0,2,"Attackers repeatedly send push notifications to users until someone approves one. Which MFA change BEST resists this and real-time phishing proxies?",["Move to FIDO2 security keys or passkeys","Switch from push to SMS one-time codes","Shorten the push approval timeout to 30 seconds","Require users to change their passwords more often"],0,"FIDO2 authenticators bind the login to the site origin and require local user action, so they resist push fatigue and phishing proxies. Number matching helps with fatigue, but SMS codes remain phishable.","Domain 2: Phishing-resistant MFA",[null,"SMS codes can be phished through a proxy site and are exposed to SIM swapping.","A shorter window still lets a tired user approve an attacker's request.","Password changes do not stop a user approving a push for an attacker who already has the password."],2],["x28",0,2,"A research agency must grant access to documents only when a user's clearance, project assignment and location all match the document's labels, and the rules change often. Which access control model BEST fits?",["Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Rule-based access control using time of day only","Attribute-based access control"],3,"ABAC evaluates policies over attributes of the user, resource and environment, so it handles combined conditions without countless roles. RBAC is tempting but leads to role explosion here.","Domain 2: Access control models",["DAC lets owners grant access at will, which cannot enforce mandatory clearance and project rules.","RBAC would need a role for every combination of clearance, project and location, causing role explosion.","Time-of-day rules consider only one condition, not user and resource attributes.",null],2],["x29",0,3,"Users on public Wi-Fi are being downgraded from HTTPS to HTTP when they type the company's domain without a scheme. Which control BEST prevents this?",["Enable HTTP/2 on the web server","Send an HSTS header and submit the domain for browser preloading","Use a longer RSA key in the site certificate","Set the Secure and HttpOnly flags on all of the site's session cookies"],1,"HSTS tells browsers to use only HTTPS for the domain, and preloading covers even the first visit, defeating SSL stripping. The Secure cookie flag is useful but does not stop the downgrade.","Domain 3: Web security headers",["HTTP/2 improves performance and does not force browsers to use HTTPS.",null,"Key length does not help if the browser never starts a TLS connection.","The Secure flag protects cookies but does not stop the page itself being loaded over HTTP."],1],["x30",0,3,"A web application has had several cross-site scripting bugs. Besides output encoding, which response header BEST limits the impact of any XSS that slips through?",["Content-Security-Policy restricting script sources","X-Frame-Options set to DENY","Strict-Transport-Security with a long max-age","Cache-Control set to no-store"],0,"A CSP that allows scripts only from trusted sources and blocks inline scripts stops most injected code from executing. X-Frame-Options is a common header but addresses clickjacking instead.","Domain 3: Application hardening",[null,"X-Frame-Options prevents clickjacking by blocking framing; it does not stop injected scripts from running.","HSTS enforces HTTPS but does nothing about scripts injected into the page.","Cache-Control governs caching, not which scripts the browser may execute."],2],["x31",0,3,"A company is planning its migration to post-quantum cryptography but does not know which applications use RSA or ECC, or where. What should it do FIRST?",["Replace every RSA certificate with 8192-bit keys, which are too large for quantum attacks to break","Disable TLS on internal networks until PQC is ready","Build an inventory of the algorithms, keys, certificates and libraries in use","Move symmetric encryption from AES-256 down to AES-128"],2,"Crypto agility starts with an inventory, sometimes called a CBOM, showing where vulnerable algorithms are used so migration can be prioritized. Larger RSA keys are tempting but do not solve the quantum threat.","Domain 3: Crypto agility",["Bigger RSA keys do not resist a cryptographically relevant quantum computer and ignore unknown uses.","Removing encryption now creates immediate risk to address a future one.",null,"AES-256 is already considered quantum-resistant; downgrading weakens it."],3],["x32",0,3,"A device maker must sign firmware updates with a NIST-standardized scheme expected to remain secure against quantum computers. Which should it choose?",["ML-KEM","ML-DSA","ECDSA with the P-384 curve","RSA-PSS with 4096-bit keys"],1,"ML-DSA (FIPS 204, derived from CRYSTALS-Dilithium) is a NIST post-quantum digital signature standard. ML-KEM is also post-quantum but is used to establish keys, not to sign.","Domain 3: Post-quantum cryptography",["ML-KEM is a post-quantum key encapsulation mechanism for key establishment, not a signature scheme.",null,"ECDSA relies on elliptic curves, which Shor's algorithm would break.","RSA relies on factoring, which a large quantum computer could solve efficiently."],3],["x33",0,3,"A code review finds that a service encrypts every message with AES-GCM using the same key and the same fixed nonce. What is the MOST serious consequence?",["Both confidentiality and message integrity can be broken","Encryption becomes slower but remains secure","The effective key length is cut in half","Messages grow larger because padding is added"],0,"Reusing a nonce under one key in GCM repeats the keystream, exposing the XOR of plaintexts, and can reveal the authentication subkey that allows forged messages. Each message needs a unique nonce.","Domain 3: Cryptographic implementation",[null,"Nonce reuse does not affect speed; it undermines the security guarantees of GCM.","The damage is not a simple key-length reduction; keystream reuse and exposure of the authentication key are the problems.","GCM works like a stream mode without padding, so message size is not the issue."],3],["x34",0,3,"A company wants to restrict which certificate authorities may issue certificates for its domain, so that a CA it does not use refuses to issue one. Which DNS record should it publish?",["TLSA","SPF","PTR","CAA"],3,"Certification Authority Authorization records list the CAs permitted to issue for a domain, and compliant CAs must check them before issuing. TLSA is tempting but applies to relying parties using DANE, not to issuance.","Domain 3: PKI controls",["TLSA (DANE) pins certificates for clients validating with DNSSEC; it does not direct CAs at issuance time.","SPF lists the servers allowed to send email for a domain, not certificate authorities.","PTR records map IP addresses back to names for reverse lookups.",null],2],["x35",0,3,"Incident responders repeatedly find that attackers dumped domain credentials from LSASS memory on Windows servers. Which hardening control BEST prevents this?",["Enable Credential Guard with virtualization-based security","Disable the Windows firewall on the servers","Increase the maximum Security log size so more credential access events are kept","Rename the built-in Administrator account"],0,"Credential Guard isolates secrets in a virtualization-protected process that ordinary reads of LSASS memory cannot reach. Renaming accounts is common advice but gives little protection against credential theft.","Domain 3: Endpoint hardening",[null,"Disabling the firewall increases exposure and does nothing to protect memory.","Larger logs retain more events but do not protect credentials from being read.","Renaming the account is minor obscurity and does not stop credential dumping from memory."],2],["x36",0,3,"Executives' laptops are often lost while traveling. The company wants stored data unreadable to a finder, whether the drive is removed or the laptop is simply powered on. Which configuration is BEST?",["Full-disk encryption protected by the TPM plus a pre-boot PIN","A strong BIOS administrator password","File-level encryption applied only to each user's Documents and Desktop folders","Full-disk encryption unlocked by the TPM alone"],0,"Full-disk encryption protects a removed drive, and a pre-boot PIN means the key is not released just by powering on. TPM-only unlock is convenient but releases the key automatically at boot.","Domain 3: Endpoint encryption",[null,"A BIOS password does not encrypt the disk, so a removed drive can be read elsewhere.","Data outside that folder, including caches and temporary files, would remain readable.","TPM-only unlock boots straight to the OS, leaving the running machine open to attack."],1],["x37",0,3,"A company wants employee laptops to join corporate Wi-Fi with mutual authentication and no shared passwords that could be phished or leaked. Which configuration is BEST?",["WPA3-Personal with a long passphrase","WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X using EAP-TLS certificates","WPA2-Enterprise with PEAP and user passwords","An open network with a captive portal login"],1,"EAP-TLS uses certificates on both sides, so there is no password to steal and each device is individually authenticated. PEAP is widely used but relies on passwords.","Domain 3: Wireless security",["A shared passphrase is still a secret users can leak, and it cannot identify individual devices.",null,"PEAP relies on passwords that can be captured if clients do not validate the server certificate.","Open networks do not encrypt the link, and a portal only adds a web login."],1],["x38",0,3,"An engineering firm is designing security for its industrial control systems and wants to group assets by criticality into zones connected through controlled conduits. Which standard series is built around this model?",["PCI DSS","ISO/IEC 27701","SOC 2","ISA/IEC 62443"],3,"ISA/IEC 62443 defines security for industrial automation and control systems using zones, conduits and security levels. ISO/IEC 27701 is also an international standard but covers privacy, not ICS.","Domain 3: OT security",["PCI DSS protects payment card data, not industrial automation systems.","ISO/IEC 27701 extends an ISMS for privacy information management.","SOC 2 is an attestation report on service organization controls, not an ICS design standard.",null],2],["x39",0,3,"A CI/CD pipeline stores a long-lived cloud access key as a secret so it can deploy. Security wants to eliminate stored cloud credentials entirely. Which approach is BEST?",["Encode the access key with base64 in the pipeline file","Rotate the access key every 90 days","Federate the pipeline via OIDC to get short-lived role credentials per job","Store the key in a private, access-controlled repository instead of the pipeline settings"],2,"Workload identity federation lets the pipeline exchange a signed OIDC token for temporary credentials scoped to a role, so no static key exists. Rotation is common practice but keeps a stored secret.","Domain 3: Pipeline secrets",["Base64 is encoding, not encryption, and the long-lived key would still exist.","Rotation shortens exposure but still leaves a stored long-lived credential.",null,"Moving the key into a repository still stores it and adds a new place it can leak from."],3],["x40",0,3,"A SOAR playbook automatically disables accounts that trigger impossible-travel alerts. The CISO worries a false positive could lock out an executive during a board meeting. What is the BEST adjustment?",["Add a human approval step before disruptive actions on high-impact accounts","Remove the playbook and handle all alerts manually","Disable impossible-travel detection for executives, since they travel frequently anyway","Run the playbook only once a week"],0,"Human-in-the-loop approval keeps automation for enrichment and low-risk actions while a person confirms business-disruptive ones. Turning detection off for executives is tempting but blinds the SOC to its most targeted users.","Domain 3: Security automation",[null,"Dropping automation throws away its speed for every low-risk case.","Executives are high-value targets, so removing detection for them increases risk.","Weekly runs would delay containment of real compromises by days."],2],["x41",0,3,"A configuration management script runs every 30 minutes. On systems already in the desired state it changes nothing, and on drifted systems it corrects only what differs. Which property does this describe?",["Non-repudiation","Obfuscation","Idempotence","Elasticity"],2,"An idempotent operation produces the same end state no matter how many times it runs, which is key to safe configuration automation. Non-repudiation is a security property but unrelated to this behavior.","Domain 3: Automation concepts",["Non-repudiation proves who performed an action; it does not describe repeatable configuration runs.","Obfuscation hides how code works and is unrelated to configuration state.",null,"Elasticity is scaling resources up and down with demand."],1],["x42",0,3,"A company wants sending mail servers to use authenticated TLS when delivering to its domain, and to refuse delivery rather than fall back to plaintext if an attacker strips STARTTLS. Which control BEST achieves this?",["Publish a DMARC record with p=reject","Publish an MTA-STS policy in enforce mode","Add more IP addresses to the SPF record","Enable DKIM signing on outbound mail"],1,"MTA-STS tells sending servers that your MX hosts support TLS with valid certificates and, in enforce mode, not to deliver if TLS fails. DMARC is tempting because it is also an email DNS record, but it addresses spoofing.","Domain 3: Email security",["DMARC deals with spoofing of your sender domain, not the encryption of mail delivered to you.",null,"SPF identifies authorized senders for your domain and has no effect on inbound TLS.","DKIM signs outgoing messages; it does not require TLS from other servers."],3],["x43",0,3,"Decommissioned SSDs that held customer data will be resold. Which sanitization method is MOST reliable for these drives?",["A single-pass overwrite of the logical volume with zeros","Cryptographic erase or the drive's built-in sanitize command","Deleting the partitions and reformatting","Degaussing each drive"],1,"SSD firmware sanitize commands and cryptographic erase cover all cells, including remapped ones, in line with NIST SP 800-88 guidance. Overwriting is tempting but misses blocks the controller hides.","Domain 3: Media sanitization",["Wear leveling and over-provisioning leave SSD blocks that a logical overwrite never reaches.",null,"Formatting removes file system structures but leaves the data blocks recoverable.","Degaussing affects magnetic media; it does not erase flash memory."],2],["x44",0,3,"An API accepts JSON Web Tokens from several client applications. Which validation approach is MOST secure?",["Verify the signature with a fixed algorithm and key, then check issuer, audience and expiry","Decode the payload and trust its claims as long as the token is well formed and base64-encoded","Accept whichever algorithm the token header specifies","Check only that the expiration time has not passed"],0,"Tokens must be verified against a fixed allowed algorithm and trusted key, with issuer, audience and time claims checked. Letting the header choose the algorithm is a well-known flaw that enables forged tokens.","Domain 3: Token security",[null,"Anyone can create a well-formed token; without verifying the signature, the claims mean nothing.","Trusting the header lets attackers choose a weaker algorithm or none, bypassing verification.","An unexpired token can still be forged or meant for a different audience."],3],["x45",0,3,"Investigators conclude that attackers obtained the KRBTGT account's key and have been forging Kerberos tickets. What remediation step is required to invalidate the forged tickets?",["Reset the passwords of all regular user accounts","Reboot every domain controller in the forest to flush all cached Kerberos tickets","Reset the KRBTGT password twice, allowing replication between resets","Disable NTLM authentication across the domain"],2,"Forged tickets are signed with the KRBTGT key, and the domain accepts both the current and previous key, so it must be reset twice to fully invalidate them. Resetting user passwords is tempting but leaves the forging key valid.","Domain 3: Identity infrastructure hardening",["User password changes do not invalidate tickets signed with the stolen KRBTGT key.","Rebooting does not change the KRBTGT key, so forged tickets remain valid.",null,"Restricting NTLM is good hardening but does not affect forged Kerberos tickets."],3],["x46",0,3,"Every Windows workstation was imaged with the same local administrator password, so one compromised machine exposes all of them. Which solution BEST fixes this?",["Change the shared password every quarter","Disable the host firewall so the helpdesk can connect","Deploy Windows LAPS for unique, rotated local admin passwords","Rename the local administrator account on all machines"],2,"LAPS gives each machine a unique, automatically rotated local admin password stored in the directory with access controls, which stops one credential from unlocking every host. Periodic rotation of a shared password is tempting but leaves it shared.","Domain 3: Credential management",["Rotating one shared password still makes it identical on every machine.","Reducing host protection adds exposure and does nothing about the shared password.",null,"A renamed account with the same password everywhere still enables lateral movement."],1],["x47",0,3,"A company is generating the root key for its new offline certificate authority and wants to ensure no single person can reconstruct or use the key. Which practice BEST achieves this?",["Store the key on an encrypted USB drive in the CISO's safe","Split the key with an M-of-N scheme across separate custodians","Email the key to two administrators for backup","Keep the key online so the CA can be restored quickly"],1,"Split knowledge with an M-of-N quorum and dual control during a witnessed key ceremony ensures no individual can use the root key alone. A single safe is tempting but leaves one point of trust.","Domain 3: Key management",["A single custodian controls the key, so one person can still use or lose it.",null,"Emailing a root key exposes it on mail systems and gives each recipient a full copy.","An online root key increases exposure and defeats the purpose of an offline root."],3],["x48",0,4,"Proxy logs show a workstation contacting an unfamiliar domain every 60 seconds, give or take a few seconds, with small similar-sized requests, around the clock. What is the MOST likely explanation?",["A user streaming video during work hours","A DNS cache poisoning attack against the resolver","Normal browser tab refreshes by an idle user","Command-and-control beaconing from malware"],3,"Regular intervals with slight jitter, small uniform payloads and activity at all hours are classic signs of C2 beaconing. User web activity would follow working hours and vary in size and destination.","Domain 4: Network analysis",["Streaming produces large, continuous transfers, not small periodic requests at all hours.","Cache poisoning corrupts DNS answers; it does not create regular outbound web requests from one host.","Browsers do not usually poll an unknown domain on a steady schedule 24 hours a day.",null],2],["x49",0,4,"An analyst sees a host making thousands of DNS queries to one domain, each with long, random-looking subdomain labels, and receiving TXT responses. What is the MOST likely activity?",["Data exfiltration or command and control through DNS tunneling","A misconfigured DHCP server on the subnet","Normal content delivery network lookups generated by a busy web browser","A DNSSEC key rollover at the registrar"],0,"Encoding data in long, high-entropy subdomains and receiving data in TXT records are signs of DNS tunneling. CDN traffic can be high volume but uses meaningful, repeated names.","Domain 4: Threat detection",[null,"DHCP issues affect address assignment, not large volumes of unusual DNS queries to one domain.","CDN lookups use a modest set of readable hostnames, not thousands of random labels.","Key rollovers happen on authoritative servers and do not make a client send random subdomain queries."],2],["x50",0,4,"Domain controller logs show one user account requesting Kerberos service tickets (event 4769) for dozens of service accounts within a minute, many with RC4 encryption. Which activity does this MOST likely indicate?",["A pass-the-hash attack reusing NTLM hashes against file servers","A normal Group Policy refresh cycle","A DHCP starvation attack","Kerberoasting to crack service account passwords offline"],3,"Kerberoasting requests service tickets encrypted with service account keys, often forcing RC4, so they can be cracked offline. Defenses include long managed service account passwords and AES-only encryption.","Domain 4: Identity attack detection",["Pass-the-hash abuses NTLM authentication, not bursts of Kerberos service ticket requests.","Group Policy refresh does not request tickets for dozens of unrelated services with RC4.","DHCP starvation exhausts IP address pools and does not appear in Kerberos logs.",null],3],["x51",0,4,"In a forensic image, a malicious file's $STANDARD_INFORMATION timestamps show 2019, but its $FILE_NAME timestamps show last week. What does this MOST likely indicate?",["The file was restored from a backup made in 2019","The system clock was set to the wrong time zone","The timestamps were deliberately altered (timestomping)","The disk image was corrupted during acquisition"],2,"User-mode tools can easily change $STANDARD_INFORMATION times but rarely $FILE_NAME times, so an SI time far older than the FN time is a classic sign of timestomping. A time zone error would move both attributes by the same few hours.","Domain 4: Forensic analysis",["A restored or copied file normally gets a new creation time in both attributes on the target volume.","A time zone error shifts times by hours, not years, and affects both attributes alike.",null,"Verified hashes would reveal corruption, and corruption would not produce such a specific pattern."],3],["x52",0,4,"A critical vulnerability affects a web application, but the vendor's patch will not be released for three weeks. What is the BEST interim action?",["Mark the finding as a false positive in the scanner until the vendor's patch arrives","Apply a virtual patch with a WAF or IPS rule for the vulnerable request pattern","Lower the CVSS score in the scanner to reduce alerting","Uninstall the scanner agent from the affected server"],1,"A virtual patch blocks exploitation at the network layer while the real fix is pending, and the exception should be tracked. Relabeling or rescoring the finding only makes the report look better.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["The vulnerability is real, so marking it false hides the risk from tracking.",null,"Changing the score does not change the exposure.","Removing monitoring hides the risk instead of reducing it."],2],["x53",0,4,"A team with limited resources wants to prioritize thousands of CVEs by the estimated probability that each will be exploited in the wild in the next 30 days. Which scoring system provides this?",["CVSS base score","CWE","EPSS","CPE"],2,"The Exploit Prediction Scoring System estimates the probability that a CVE will be exploited within 30 days. CVSS is tempting but measures severity, and many high-scoring CVEs are rarely exploited.","Domain 4: Vulnerability prioritization",["CVSS base scores rate technical severity, not the likelihood of exploitation.","CWE is a catalog of weakness types, not a score for specific CVEs.",null,"CPE is a naming scheme for products and platforms."],2],["x54",0,4,"An external firm is hired to run a penetration test against production systems next week. What must be in place BEFORE testing begins?",["A completed remediation plan for the findings","Signed authorization with an agreed scope and rules of engagement","Disabled logging so the test does not trigger alerts","Domain admin credentials for every system in the company, including production databases"],1,"Written authorization, scope, timing, contacts and rules of engagement protect both parties and prevent out-of-scope harm. Disabling logging is sometimes suggested to reduce noise but removes detection and accountability.","Domain 4: Security testing",["Findings do not exist yet, so remediation is planned after the test.",null,"Disabling logging hides real attacks and removes evidence of what testers did.","Access depends on the test type and scope; blanket admin rights are not a prerequisite."],1],["x55",0,4,"Responders discover a sophisticated intruder with footholds on several hosts, and the full extent of access is not yet known. Leadership wants to reimage the first host found immediately. What is the BEST approach?",["Reimage the first host right away and keep monitoring the rest for further suspicious activity","Keep scoping quietly, then remove all footholds in one coordinated eradication","Announce the breach publicly before investigating further","Hire a third party to attack the intruder's servers"],1,"Against an advanced intruder, acting on one host tips them off; full scoping followed by simultaneous eradication cuts off all access at once. Urgent containment may still be needed if damage is ongoing, but piecemeal cleanup is the common mistake.","Domain 4: Incident response",["Removing one foothold alerts the intruder, who may use others or change tactics.",null,"Premature disclosure can tip off the attacker and may be inaccurate.","Hacking back is typically illegal and does not remove the intruder from your network."],3],["x56",0,4,"A threat intelligence analyst describes an intrusion by linking the adversary, its capabilities, the infrastructure it used and the victim. Which analytic model is this?",["The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain","STRIDE","The Diamond Model","The CIA triad"],2,"The Diamond Model relates adversary, capability, infrastructure and victim for each event, helping analysts pivot between them. The Cyber Kill Chain is also an intrusion model but is organized by phases.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",["The kill chain describes sequential attack stages, not four linked features.","STRIDE classifies threat types during design-time threat modeling.",null,"The CIA triad names security objectives, not an intrusion analysis model."],1],["x57",0,4,"An attacker used a stolen cloud access key to create new compute instances and change storage permissions. Which log source is MOST useful to reconstruct these actions?",["The provider's control-plane audit logs of API calls","Operating system logs inside an existing instance","Network flow logs of the virtual network","Access logs from the public web server"],0,"Control-plane audit logs record each management API call with identity, time, source address and parameters. Flow logs are tempting but only show network connections.","Domain 4: Cloud investigations",[null,"Guest OS logs do not record API calls made to the provider to create resources.","Flow logs show connection metadata, not which management actions were performed.","Web access logs record requests to the application, not management actions in the cloud account."],2],["x58",0,4,"EDR shows a legitimate process making unusual network connections, but no malicious file exists on disk. Memory analysis is planned. Which finding would MOST strongly indicate process injection?",["Executable read-write memory regions with no backing file on disk","The process has a valid digital signature from the OS vendor","The process runs from the System32 directory","The process holds a large number of open file handles"],0,"Private executable memory not mapped from any image file, often with RWX permissions, is a classic sign of injected code. A trusted signature is misleading because injection hides inside trusted processes.","Domain 4: Memory forensics",[null,"Injected code runs inside legitimately signed processes, so a valid signature does not rule it out.","A legitimate path is exactly what injection abuses; it is not evidence either way.","Many handles are normal for busy processes and do not point to injected code."],3],["x59",0,4,"During a purple team exercise, several emulated attacker techniques ran without triggering any SIEM alert. What is the BEST ongoing practice to catch such gaps?",["Lower all alert thresholds to their minimum","Run regular attack simulations mapped to ATT&CK and track detection coverage","Buy a second SIEM to run in parallel","Rely on the SIEM vendor's default rule packs and wait for their quarterly content updates"],1,"Breach and attack simulation or recurring purple team tests confirm that detections actually fire, turning missed techniques into tracked detection engineering work. Lowering thresholds is tempting but trades gaps for alert fatigue.","Domain 4: Detection engineering",["Lowering every threshold floods analysts with false positives without confirming coverage.",null,"A second SIEM with the same data and rules would have the same gaps.","Default content is not tuned to your environment and will not reveal your specific blind spots."],2],["x60",0,4,"A scanner flags a vulnerable library in a vendor appliance. The vendor supplies a machine-readable statement that the vulnerable function is not reachable in its product, so the product is not affected. What is this document called?",["An SBOM","A CVE record","A SOC 2 Type II report","A VEX statement"],3,"A Vulnerability Exploitability eXchange (VEX) document lets a supplier state whether a product is affected by a given vulnerability, which helps teams suppress non-exploitable findings. An SBOM is closely related but only inventories components.","Domain 4: Vulnerability management",["An SBOM lists components; it does not state whether a vulnerability is exploitable in the product.","A CVE record describes the vulnerability itself, not whether a specific product is affected.","A SOC 2 report attests to service organization controls, not per-vulnerability exploitability.",null],3]]);
